                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me in what substance do clouds float?\n\nProfessor: Sure, clouds float in a substance called air.\n\nStudent: What is air made up of?\n\nProfessor: Air is made up of a mixture of gases, primarily nitrogen and oxygen.\n\nStudent: How does the air hold up the clouds?\n\nProfessor: The air holds up the clouds because the clouds are less dense than the surrounding air. This causes them to float and move with the wind.\n\nStudent: So, if the air is holding up the clouds, why don't they fall down?\n\nProfessor: The clouds don't fall down because the air pressure below the cloud is greater than the air pressure inside the cloud. This creates an upward force that keeps the cloud afloat.\n\nStudent: I see. Thank you for explaining that to me, professor.\n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Do you have any other questions?",
