                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Saliva is a clear liquid that is produced by the salivary glands in the mouth. It helps to moisten and break down food, as well as protect the teeth and gums from bacteria.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What causes excessive saliva production?\n2. What are the potential health risks associated with excessive saliva production?\n3. How can excessive saliva production be treated or managed?\n\nAnswer:\n1. Excessive saliva production can be caused by a variety of factors, including medication side effects, neurological disorders, and certain medical conditions such as gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD).\n2. While excessive saliva production is generally not harmful, it can be a symptom of an underlying health issue. For example, GERD can cause damage to the esophagus if left untreated. Additionally, excessive drooling can lead to skin irritation and infection around the mouth.\n3. Treatment for excessive saliva production depends on the underlying cause. In some cases, medication adjustments or therapy may be necessary. In other cases, lifestyle changes such as avoiding certain foods or practicing good oral hygiene may be helpful. In severe cases, surgery may be required to remove the salivary glands.",
