                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what a glacial lake is. A glacial lake is a body of water that forms in a basin created by a glacier. Glaciers are large masses of ice that move slowly over land, carving out valleys and creating depressions in the landscape. When a glacier melts, it can leave behind a depression that fills with water, creating a glacial lake.\n\nBased on this definition, we can eliminate options A, B, and C (Michigan and New York) as they do not have significant mountain ranges or glaciers. Option E (countryside) is also unlikely to have glacial lakes as it does not have the necessary topography.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D (mountains), as mountainous regions are more likely to have glaciers and therefore, glacial lakes. Examples of glacial lakes in mountainous regions include Lake Louise in Canada and Lake Tahoe in the United States.",
