                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: What is a glacial lake?\nA glacial lake is a body of water that forms in a basin created by a glacier. Glaciers can carve out depressions in the landscape, which can then fill with water to form a lake.\n\nStep 2: Which of the options listed could have been formed by a glacier?\nA) Michigan - Michigan has many lakes, but not all of them were formed by glaciers. However, some of the larger lakes in Michigan, such as Lake Michigan and Lake Huron, were formed by glaciers during the last ice age.\nB) New York - New York also has many lakes, but not all of them were formed by glaciers. However, the Finger Lakes region of New York was formed by glaciers and contains many glacial lakes.\nC) New York - This is a duplicate option.\nD) Mountains - Glaciers can form in mountainous regions, but not all lakes in mountains were formed by glaciers. However, some mountain lakes, such as Lake Louise in Canada, were formed by glaciers.\nE) Countryside - This option is too broad and does not provide enough information to determine if a lake was formed by a glacier.\n\nStep 3: Conclusion\nBased on the information above, options A and B are both likely to have glacial lakes. Therefore, the correct answer is either A) Michigan or B) New York.",
