                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Glacial lakes are formed by the melting of glaciers and are typically found in areas that have experienced glacial activity.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the characteristics of a glacial lake?\n2. What areas have experienced glacial activity?\n3. What are the geographical features of Michigan, New York, mountains, and countryside?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Glacial lakes are typically deep, clear, and cold. They may have a distinctive blue or green color due to the presence of glacial flour (fine rock particles) in the water.\n2. Glacial activity has occurred in many parts of the world, including North America, Europe, and Asia. During the last ice age, which ended about 10,000 years ago, much of North America was covered by glaciers.\n3. Michigan and New York are both states in the northeastern United States that were affected by glacial activity during the last ice age. Mountains are often associated with glacial activity, as glaciers can carve out valleys and create lakes in high-altitude areas. Countryside is a more general term that could refer to many different types of landscapes, some of which may have been affected by glacial activity. \n\nBased on this information, the most likely answer to the original question is D) mountains, as glacial lakes are often found in high-altitude areas that have experienced glacial activity. However, it is also possible that glacial lakes could be found in Michigan or New York, as these states were affected by glacial activity during the last ice age. The answer E) countryside is too broad to make a definitive statement about the likelihood of finding a glacial lake.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "d057ea30-5d09-43c8-bc2d-d9cde5ee0d01",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
