                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Snowflakes are formed when water vapor in the air freezes into ice crystals.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is required for water vapor to freeze into ice crystals?\n2. Where can water vapor be found in the atmosphere?\n3. What conditions are necessary for the formation of snowflakes?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Water vapor needs to be at a temperature below freezing (32\u00b0F or 0\u00b0C) and needs a surface to freeze onto, such as a dust particle or a speck of dirt.\n2. Water vapor can be found in the atmosphere in the form of moisture, such as humidity or fog.\n3. Snowflakes form in clouds when the temperature is below freezing and there is enough moisture in the air for the water vapor to freeze onto particles. The ice crystals then grow and combine to form snowflakes. Snowstorms are a result of these snowflakes falling to the ground. \n\nTherefore, the answer to the original question is A) cloud.",
