                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me where a snowflake forms?\n\nProfessor: Sure, a snowflake forms in a cloud.\n\nStudent: How does it form in a cloud?\n\nProfessor: Well, when water vapor in the air cools down and condenses into tiny droplets, they freeze into ice crystals. These ice crystals then stick together and form a snowflake.\n\nStudent: So, the snowflake forms from ice crystals?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The ice crystals are the building blocks of a snowflake.\n\nStudent: Does it only form in a cloud?\n\nProfessor: Yes, a snowflake can only form in a cloud because it requires the right temperature and moisture conditions.\n\nStudent: What about a snowstorm? Can a snowflake form during a snowstorm?\n\nProfessor: Yes, a snowstorm is actually a collection of snowflakes falling from the sky. So, a snowflake can form during a snowstorm, but it still originates from a cloud.",
