                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has decreased urinary output and malaise two weeks after undergoing an emergency cardiac catherization with stenting for unstable angina pectoris. This suggests a possible complication related to the procedure or the medications given after the procedure.\n\nThe patient has type 2 diabetes mellitus and osteoarthritis of the hips, which are chronic conditions that may increase the risk of complications.\n\nThe patient's medications include insulin and naproxen, which may affect renal function.\n\nThe patient was also started on aspirin, clopidogrel, and metoprolol after the coronary intervention, which may have additional effects on renal function.\n\nThe patient has a temperature of 38\u00b0C (100.4\u00b0F), which suggests an inflammatory process.\n\nThe patient has mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet, which may be a sign of peripheral vascular disease or embolization.\n\nThe laboratory studies show leukocytosis with a left shift, eosinophilia, and an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, which suggest an inflammatory or allergic process.\n\nThe serum urea nitrogen and creatinine levels are elevated, which suggest renal dysfunction.\n\nThe renal biopsy shows intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles, which are consistent with cholesterol embolization.\n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is C) Cholesterol embolization, which is a complication of cardiac catheterization and stenting that can lead to renal dysfunction, peripheral vascular disease, and other systemic manifestations. The patient's chronic conditions and medications may have contributed to the development of this complication.",
                        "answers": [
