                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient underwent emergency cardiac catherization with stenting for unstable angina pectoris two weeks ago. He has type 2 diabetes mellitus and osteoarthritis of the hips. He is currently taking insulin, naproxen, aspirin, clopidogrel, and metoprolol. He presents with decreased urinary output, malaise, and mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet. Laboratory studies show elevated leukocyte count, eosinophils, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate, as well as elevated serum urea nitrogen and creatinine. Renal biopsy shows intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the significance of the patient's medical history and current medications?\n2. What are the possible causes of decreased urinary output and malaise?\n3. What is the significance of the mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet?\n4. What do the laboratory studies indicate?\n5. What is the significance of the intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles seen on renal biopsy?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's medical history and current medications are important because they can provide clues to the cause of his symptoms. Type 2 diabetes mellitus and osteoarthritis of the hips may increase his risk for certain conditions. Insulin and naproxen may have side effects that affect the kidneys. Aspirin, clopidogrel, and metoprolol are medications commonly used after cardiac catherization with stenting.\n2. Decreased urinary output and malaise can be caused by a variety of conditions, including kidney damage, dehydration, infection, and medication side effects.\n3. The mottled, reticulated purplish discoloration of the feet may be a sign of peripheral vascular disease or cholesterol embolization.\n4. The laboratory studies indicate elevated leukocyte count, eosinophils, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate, as well as elevated serum urea nitrogen and creatinine. These findings suggest inflammation and possible kidney damage.\n5. The intravascular spindle-shaped vacuoles seen on renal biopsy are consistent with cholesterol embolization, which occurs when cholesterol crystals break off from atherosclerotic plaques and lodge in small blood vessels, causing inflammation and damage. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is C) Cholesterol embolization.",
