                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What lab finding would most likely be seen in a 4-year-old boy with vomiting, diarrhea, bloody stools, decreased oral intake, and lower extremity weakness and tingling?\n\nStep-by-step:\n1. The patient is a 4-year-old boy with vomiting, diarrhea, bloody stools, and decreased oral intake.\n2. He also has lower extremity weakness and tingling.\n3. On physical exam, he has a fever, high blood pressure, and dry mucous membranes.\n4. Abdominal exam shows diffuse tenderness without rebound or guarding.\n5. Stool is guaiac positive.\n6. Acetaminophen and fluids are started.\n7. The next day, he has lower extremity weakness and tingling with diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes.\n8. We are asked which lab finding would most likely be seen in this patient.\n\nAnswer: D) Increased cerebrospinal fluid protein with normal cell count\n\nExplanation: The patient's symptoms and physical exam findings suggest a possible diagnosis of Guillain-Barre syndrome, which is a rare but serious autoimmune disorder that affects the peripheral nervous system. The lower extremity weakness and tingling, along with diminished patellar deep tendon reflexes, are consistent with this diagnosis. The most common lab finding in Guillain-Barre syndrome is an increased cerebrospinal fluid protein level with a normal cell count. Gram stain positive CSF, peripheral eosinophilia, xanthochromia on cerebrospinal fluid analysis, and oligoclonal bands on cerebrospinal fluid analysis are not typically associated with Guillain-Barre syndrome.",
