                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The newborn is presenting with poor weight gain, irritability, and a swollen neck at the midline. The mother has a history of Graves' disease and underwent thyroidectomy during pregnancy. The newborn's vital signs are also elevated, with a high temperature, pulse, and respiratory rate. These symptoms suggest hyperthyroidism in the newborn, which could be caused by transplacental passage of thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) from the mother's Graves' disease. Therefore, the most likely cause is C) Transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies. \n\nTo confirm the diagnosis, thyroid function tests should be performed, including TSH, free T4, and TSI levels. Treatment may include antithyroid medication or radioactive iodine therapy. Close monitoring of the newborn's growth and development is also necessary.",
