                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely cause of poor weight gain in a 3-week-old male newborn with a history of Graves' disease in the mother?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The patient is a 3-week-old male newborn with poor weight gain since birth.\n2. The mother has a history of Graves' disease and underwent near-total thyroidectomy in the second trimester of her pregnancy.\n3. The patient's temperature is 38.9\u00b0C (102\u00b0F), pulse is 176/min, and respirations are 42/min. He appears irritable.\n4. Examination shows a diaphoretic infant with a paucity of subcutaneous fat. There is swelling of the neck at the midline.\n5. The question is asking for the most likely cause of the poor weight gain in this patient.\n\nAnswer: C) Transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies\n\nReasoning: The patient's mother has a history of Graves' disease, which is an autoimmune disorder that causes the production of antibodies against the TSH receptor. These antibodies can cross the placenta and stimulate the fetal thyroid gland, leading to hyperthyroidism in the newborn. Hyperthyroidism can cause poor weight gain, irritability, and other symptoms seen in this patient. Therefore, the most likely cause of the poor weight gain in this patient is transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies.",
