                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The most likely cause of the 3-week-old male newborn's poor weight gain and other symptoms is being asked. It is important to note that the mother has a history of Graves' disease and underwent near-total thyroidectomy in the second trimester of her pregnancy.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Graves' disease?\n2. What is a thyroidectomy?\n3. What is L-thyroxine therapy?\n4. What are the symptoms of hyperthyroidism in newborns?\n5. What are the possible causes of the newborn's symptoms?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder that causes hyperthyroidism, which is an overactive thyroid gland.\n2. A thyroidectomy is the surgical removal of all or part of the thyroid gland.\n3. L-thyroxine therapy is a medication used to replace or supplement the thyroid hormone in the body.\n4. Symptoms of hyperthyroidism in newborns include poor weight gain, irritability, tachycardia (fast heart rate), tachypnea (fast breathing), and a goiter (swelling of the thyroid gland).\n5. The most likely cause of the newborn's symptoms is transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies from the mother with a history of Graves' disease. This can cause neonatal hyperthyroidism, which can lead to poor weight gain and other symptoms. The swelling of the neck at the midline is consistent with a goiter. The other answer choices are less likely to be the cause of the newborn's symptoms based on the information provided.",
