                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what Graves' disease is and how it can affect the baby?\n\nProfessor: Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder that causes the thyroid gland to produce too much thyroid hormone. This excess hormone can cross the placenta and affect the baby's thyroid gland, causing it to produce too much thyroid hormone as well. This can lead to a condition called neonatal hyperthyroidism, which can cause poor weight gain, irritability, and other symptoms.\n\nStudent: What is a near-total thyroidectomy?\n\nProfessor: A near-total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure in which most of the thyroid gland is removed. This is often done in cases of Graves' disease that do not respond to other treatments, such as antithyroid drugs.\n\nStudent: What is L-thyroxine therapy?\n\nProfessor: L-thyroxine is a synthetic form of thyroid hormone that is used to treat hypothyroidism, which is a condition in which the thyroid gland does not produce enough thyroid hormone. In this case, the mother is receiving L-thyroxine therapy to replace the thyroid hormone that her body is not producing due to the thyroidectomy.\n\nStudent: Can you explain the options for the most likely cause of the baby's symptoms?\n\nProfessor: Option A refers to the transplacental passage of thyroglobulin antibodies, which can cause neonatal hypothyroidism. Option B refers to the transplacental passage of thyroid peroxidase antibodies, which can also cause neonatal hypothyroidism. Option C refers to the transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies, which can cause neonatal hyperthyroidism. Option D refers to transplacental viral infection, which can cause a variety of symptoms in the baby. Option E refers to opiate use in the mother, which can also cause a variety of symptoms in the baby. Based on the baby's symptoms and the mother's history of Graves' disease, option C (transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies) is the most likely cause.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8ca069bd-f041-4e1c-bd0e-64b965fc586a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
