                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the case.\n\n- 3-week-old male newborn\n- Poor weight gain since birth\n- Born at 38 weeks' gestation via normal vaginal delivery\n- Weighed 3005 g (6 lb, 10 oz) at birth and currently weighs 2835 g (6 lb, 4 oz)\n- Breastfeeding well since birth\n- Mother has a history of Graves' disease and underwent near-total thyroidectomy in the second trimester of her pregnancy\n- Mother is currently receiving L-thyroxine therapy\n- Patient's temperature is 38.9\u00b0C (102\u00b0F), pulse is 176/min, and respirations are 42/min\n- Infant appears irritable\n- Examination shows a diaphoretic infant with a paucity of subcutaneous fat\n- There is swelling of the neck at the midline\n\nStep 2: Formulate an initial impression.\n\nThe newborn's poor weight gain, along with the presence of a goiter and symptoms of hyperthyroidism, suggest that the most likely cause is congenital hyperthyroidism. The mother's history of Graves' disease and thyroidectomy further support this diagnosis.\n\nStep 3: Gather more information to confirm or refute the initial impression.\n\nTo confirm the diagnosis of congenital hyperthyroidism, further testing would be needed to measure the newborn's thyroid hormone levels and TSH receptor antibodies. However, given the clinical presentation and maternal history, this is the most likely cause.\n\nStep 4: Consider other possible diagnoses.\n\nTransplacental passage of thyroglobulin antibodies, thyroid peroxidase antibodies, or viral infection could also cause thyroid dysfunction in the newborn, but these are less likely given the maternal history of Graves' disease. Opiate use in the mother could also affect the newborn's weight gain, but it would not explain the other symptoms.\n\nStep 5: Formulate a final impression.\n\nThe most likely cause of the newborn's poor weight gain, goiter, and symptoms of hyperthyroidism is congenital hyperthyroidism due to transplacental passage of TSH receptor antibodies from the mother with a history of Graves' disease and thyroidectomy.",
