                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient in this scenario has a penetrating wound to the chest with profuse bleeding and a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall. The best definitive surgical approach to take in this patient would be to perform an interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets. This approach would allow for repair of the injury to the right ventricular free wall and control of the bleeding. Immediate cardiac transplant would not be feasible in this scenario as it is a time-consuming and complex procedure that would not address the immediate need for repair of the injury and control of bleeding. Watchful waiting while resuscitative fluids are initiated would not be appropriate as the patient is already in a critical condition and requires immediate intervention. Needle thoracostomy over the 2nd intercostal space would not be appropriate as it is a procedure used to relieve tension pneumothorax, which is not the primary concern in this scenario. Surgical adhesive glue would not be appropriate as it would not provide adequate repair of the injury and control of bleeding.",
