                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the best definitive surgical approach for a 25-year-old male with a penetrating wound to the chest and a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has a penetrating wound to the chest and is unconscious, indicating a serious injury.\n- Vitals show low blood pressure, high pulse rate, and low oxygen saturation, indicating shock.\n- Physical examination reveals profuse bleeding from the wound and a full thickness penetrating injury to the right ventricular free wall, indicating a life-threatening condition.\n- The patient is intubated and aggressive fluid resuscitation is initiated, including a blood transfusion, to stabilize the patient's condition.\n- The question asks for the best definitive surgical approach, which means a procedure that can repair the injury and prevent further complications.\n\nAnswer: C) Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets\n- This option involves suturing the wound with a strong material (polypropylene) and using pledgets (small pads) to support the suture and prevent tearing of the heart tissue.\n- This approach is appropriate for a full thickness penetrating injury to the heart, as it can repair the damage and prevent further bleeding.\n- Immediate cardiac transplant (A) is not feasible in this situation, as it requires a suitable donor heart and time for the procedure, which the patient may not have.\n- Watchful waiting (B) is not appropriate, as the patient is in shock and needs immediate intervention.\n- Needle thoracostomy (D) is a procedure to relieve tension pneumothorax, which is not indicated in this case.\n- Surgical adhesive glue (E) is not appropriate for a full thickness penetrating injury to the heart, as it cannot provide sufficient support and may not seal the wound completely.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "28d709e4-a3b4-431d-a1b4-c23cc58072c8",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
