                    {
                        "id": "87508a15-d6f1-480c-8001-5aedd7d8be13",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a penetrating wound is?\n\nProfessor: A penetrating wound is a type of injury that occurs when an object, such as a knife or bullet, enters the body and damages internal organs or tissues.\n\nStudent: Why is the patient unconscious?\n\nProfessor: It's possible that the patient lost consciousness due to the severity of the injury and the resulting shock to the body.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when the patient's extremities are pale and cool?\n\nProfessor: Pale and cool extremities can be a sign of poor blood flow to the limbs, which can occur when the body is in shock.\n\nStudent: What is transthoracic echocardiography?\n\nProfessor: Transthoracic echocardiography is a type of imaging test that uses sound waves to create pictures of the heart and its surrounding structures.\n\nStudent: Why is aggressive fluid resuscitation necessary?\n\nProfessor: Aggressive fluid resuscitation is necessary to help stabilize the patient's blood pressure and improve blood flow to vital organs.\n\nStudent: What is a definitive surgical approach?\n\nProfessor: A definitive surgical approach is a surgical procedure that is intended to definitively treat a patient's condition, rather than just providing temporary relief.\n\nStudent: Why is a cardiac transplant not the best option in this case?\n\nProfessor: A cardiac transplant is not the best option in this case because it is a very invasive procedure that is typically reserved for patients with end-stage heart failure. Additionally, the patient's injury is not related to heart failure.\n\nStudent: Why is interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets the best option?\n\nProfessor: Interrupted 2-0 polypropylene suture with supporting pledgets is the best option because it allows for precise closure of the penetrating wound and provides good hemostasis (control of bleeding). It is also a relatively simple and quick procedure that can be performed in an emergency setting.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "544f5958-ad98-4146-b865-c0b38d8aa285",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
