                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Melanoma is a type of skin cancer that arises from pigment-producing cells called melanocytes. There are several subtypes of melanoma, including superficial spreading, nodular, lentigo maligna, acral lentiginous, and desmoplastic. Risk factors for melanoma include fair skin, a history of sunburns, a family history of melanoma, and a weakened immune system.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the risk factors for melanoma?\nAnswer: Fair skin, a history of sunburns, a family history of melanoma, and a weakened immune system are all risk factors for melanoma.\n\n2. What are the subtypes of melanoma?\nAnswer: The subtypes of melanoma include superficial spreading, nodular, lentigo maligna, acral lentiginous, and desmoplastic.\n\n3. Which subtype of melanoma is most common in African Americans?\nAnswer: Acral lentiginous melanoma is the most common subtype of melanoma in African Americans.\n\nAnswer: Based on the information provided, the woman has no abnormal moles or warts on her skin, but she does have a family history of metastatic melanoma. While it is not possible to determine her exact risk of developing melanoma, she may be at increased risk due to her family history. African Americans are more likely to develop acral lentiginous melanoma, which occurs on the palms of the hands, soles of the feet, or under the nails. Therefore, the woman is at greatest risk of developing acral lentiginous melanoma (option C).",
