                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "D) Natural killer cells are a class of immune cells that can lyse abnormal cells, including tumor cells and virus-infected cells, without the need for opsonization, priming, or prior activation. They are able to recognize and kill cells that have downregulated class I major histocompatibility molecules, which is a common mechanism used by tumor cells to evade recognition by the immune system. CD8+ T lymphocytes also have the ability to recognize and kill abnormal cells, but they require prior activation and recognition of antigen presented on class I major histocompatibility molecules. Bone marrow-derived macrophages and band neutrophils are not involved in this process. CD4+ T lymphocytes primarily function in the adaptive immune response by providing help to other immune cells.",
