                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\nThe best next step in management is E) Inferior petrosal sinus sampling. This test is used to differentiate between primary and secondary causes of Cushing's syndrome. It involves measuring the levels of ACTH in the inferior petrosal sinuses and comparing them to the levels in the peripheral veins. If the ACTH levels are higher in the petrosal sinuses, it suggests a pituitary source of ACTH, indicating Cushing's disease. If the ACTH levels are similar in both locations, it suggests an ectopic source of ACTH, indicating Cushing's syndrome.",
