                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nE) Inferior petrosal sinus sampling\n\nThis patient's presentation is consistent with Cushing's syndrome, which is characterized by excess cortisol production. The high serum cortisol levels, lack of suppression with high dose dexamethasone, and low ACTH levels suggest an adrenal source of excess cortisol production (ACTH-independent Cushing's syndrome). The next step in management would be to localize the source of excess cortisol production. Inferior petrosal sinus sampling is the most accurate test for distinguishing between pituitary and ectopic sources of ACTH production. MRI of the adrenal glands or chest may be considered if the inferior petrosal sinus sampling is inconclusive, but they are less sensitive and specific than the sampling. MRI of the pituitary gland would not be helpful in this case, as the ACTH levels are low and suggest an adrenal source of excess cortisol production. Low dose dexamethasone suppression test is not necessary, as the high dose test has already shown lack of suppression.",
                        "answers": [
