                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's presentation and laboratory findings are consistent with Cushing's syndrome, which is characterized by excess cortisol production. The high 24-hour urinary cortisol and serum cortisol levels, as well as the lack of suppression with a high dose dexamethasone suppression test, suggest that the source of excess cortisol is likely adrenal in origin. \n\nTherefore, the best next step in management would be to perform an MRI of the adrenal glands (option A) to look for any adrenal masses or tumors that may be causing the excess cortisol production. An MRI of the chest (option B) would be indicated if there was suspicion for a bronchial carcinoid tumor, which can also cause excess cortisol production. An MRI of the pituitary gland (option C) would be indicated if there was suspicion for Cushing's disease, which is caused by excess adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) production from a pituitary adenoma. However, the low serum ACTH level in this patient makes Cushing's disease less likely. \n\nA low dose dexamethasone suppression test (option D) would not be helpful in this case, as the patient has already failed a high dose suppression test. Inferior petrosal sinus sampling (option E) is a more invasive test that is used to distinguish between Cushing's disease and ectopic ACTH production, but is not necessary in this case given the low serum ACTH level. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) MRI of the adrenal glands.",
                        "answers": [
