                        "cot": "it is important to note that the intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces and no identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid is likely a molar pregnancy. \n\nThe most likely cause of the ovarian findings in this case is E) Theca lutein cysts. These cysts are associated with high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which is produced by molar pregnancies. The elevated levels of hCG stimulate the ovaries to produce multiple cysts, which are typically thin-walled and septated with clear content. \n\nChocolate cysts (A) are typically associated with endometriosis, while corpus luteum cysts (B) are a normal part of the menstrual cycle and usually resolve on their own. Yolk sac tumors (C) are a type of ovarian germ cell tumor and luteomas of pregnancy (D) are rare benign tumors that can occur during pregnancy. Neither of these conditions are associated with the findings described in this case.",
