                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's analyze the ultrasound findings:\n\n1. Intrauterine heteroechoic mass with numerous anechoic spaces: This suggests a molar pregnancy (hydatidiform mole), which is an abnormal growth of placental tissue.\n2. No identifiable fetus or amniotic fluid: This further supports the diagnosis of a molar pregnancy.\n3. Enlarged ovaries with multiple thin-walled, septated cysts with clear content: This finding is suggestive of ovarian cysts, but we need to determine the specific type.\n\nNow, let's go through the answer choices:\n\nA) Chocolate cysts: These are endometriomas, which are related to endometriosis. They are not typically associated with molar pregnancies.\n\nB) Corpus luteum cysts: These cysts can occur during normal pregnancies, but they are not typically associated with molar pregnancies.\n\nC) Yolk sac tumor: This is a type of germ cell tumor, which is not related to molar pregnancies.\n\nD) Luteoma of pregnancy: These are benign ovarian masses that can occur during pregnancy, but they are not typically associated with molar pregnancies.\n\nE) Theca lutein cysts: These cysts are associated with high levels of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which is commonly seen in molar pregnancies. Theca lutein cysts are the most likely cause of the ovarian findings in this case.\n\nSo, the correct answer is E) Theca lutein cysts.",
