                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 35-year-old Caucasian female with a history of a thrombus in the right femoral vein and now presents with unilateral leg swelling and redness. She has no recent prolonged travel and is otherwise healthy. Ultrasound reveals a thrombus in the left popliteal vein, and a genetic workup reveals an inherited condition.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the risk factors for developing a thrombus?\n2. What is the difference between a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) and a pulmonary embolism (PE)?\n3. What is protein C and what is its role in the coagulation cascade?\n4. What is the role of thrombin inhibitors in preventing clot formation?\n5. What is homocysteine and how does it relate to thrombus formation?\n6. What are phospholipids and how do auto-antibodies directed against them contribute to thrombus formation?\n7. What is resistance to clotting factor degradation and how does it contribute to thrombus formation?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Risk factors for developing a thrombus include immobility, surgery, trauma, cancer, pregnancy, hormonal contraceptives, smoking, obesity, and genetic factors.\n2. A DVT is a blood clot that forms in a deep vein, usually in the leg, while a PE is a blood clot that travels to the lungs and blocks blood flow. Both are serious conditions that require prompt medical attention.\n3. Protein C is a natural anticoagulant that helps regulate the coagulation cascade by inactivating factors V and VIII, which are involved in clot formation.\n4. Thrombin inhibitors are medications that prevent the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin, which is necessary for clot formation.\n5. Homocysteine is an amino acid that, when elevated, can damage the lining of blood vessels and increase the risk of thrombus formation.\n6. Phospholipids are a type of fat that are present in cell membranes and play a role in blood clotting. Auto-antibodies directed against phospholipids can interfere with the normal clotting process and increase the risk of thrombus formation.\n7. Resistance to clotting factor degradation is a genetic condition in which the body is unable to break down clotting factors, leading to an increased risk of thrombus formation.\n\nBased on the patient's history and genetic workup, the most likely pathophysiology of her condition is C) Deficiency in protein C, which",
