                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a mischarged amino acid is?\n\nProfessor: Sure. A mischarged amino acid is when the wrong amino acid is attached to a tRNA molecule. In this case, the Lys-tRNA is charged with valine instead of lysine.\n\nStudent: What is the purpose of inserting the mischarged tRNA into the cell?\n\nProfessor: The purpose is to see if the ribosome can recognize the mischarged tRNA and prevent its incorporation into the growing peptide.\n\nStudent: What is the mRNA sequence designed for?\n\nProfessor: The mRNA sequence is designed to contain only codons for lysine, which is the amino acid that the ribosome is supposed to incorporate into the growing peptide.\n\nStudent: So, what do you think will happen in this experiment?\n\nProfessor: Based on the options given, the most likely scenario is that the mischarged tRNA with valine will be incorporated in the codons that specify for lysine. Therefore, the answer is C.",
