                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Metastatic lung cancer can cause severe pain that requires opioid medication for management. However, escalating doses of opioids can lead to dose-limiting side effects. Medications that interact with the NMDA-receptor can reduce the need for opioids and improve pain management.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the NMDA-receptor?\n2. How does interaction with the NMDA-receptor reduce the need for opioids?\n3. Which of the listed medications interacts with the NMDA-receptor?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The NMDA-receptor is a type of glutamate receptor that plays a role in pain transmission and modulation in the central nervous system.\n2. Interaction with the NMDA-receptor can reduce the need for opioids by enhancing the analgesic effects of opioids and reducing the development of tolerance to opioids.\n3. The most likely recommended agent is B) Ketamine, as it is a medication that interacts with the NMDA-receptor and has been shown to be effective in reducing opioid use and improving pain management in cancer patients. A) Propofol is a sedative medication that does not interact with the NMDA-receptor. C) Fentanyl is an opioid medication that does not interact with the NMDA-receptor. D) Ketorolac is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug that does not interact with the NMDA-receptor. E) Midazolam is a sedative medication that does not interact with the NMDA-receptor.",
