                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's identify the condition described in the question. The absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae, along with the small pelvis, shallow intergluteal cleft, and club feet, suggest a diagnosis of caudal regression syndrome (also known as sacral agenesis).\n\nNow let's go through the answer choices:\n\nA) Maternal oligohydramnios: Oligohydramnios is a condition where there is not enough amniotic fluid surrounding the fetus. It can cause various complications, but it is not the strongest predisposing factor for caudal regression syndrome.\n\nB) Intrauterine alcohol exposure: Alcohol exposure during pregnancy can lead to a range of birth defects, known as fetal alcohol spectrum disorders. However, it is not the strongest predisposing factor for caudal regression syndrome.\n\nC) Chromosome 13 trisomy: Trisomy 13, also known as Patau syndrome, is a chromosomal disorder that causes severe intellectual disability and physical abnormalities. It is not specifically associated with caudal regression syndrome.\n\nD) Maternal diabetes: Maternal diabetes, particularly pregestational diabetes, is a well-established risk factor for caudal regression syndrome. Infants born to mothers with diabetes have an increased risk of developing this condition.\n\nE) Chromosome 18 trisomy: Trisomy 18, also known as Edwards syndrome, is a chromosomal disorder that causes severe intellectual disability and physical abnormalities. It is not specifically associated with caudal regression syndrome.\n\nBased on this information, the strongest predisposing factor for this patient's condition is:\n\nD) Maternal diabetes",
