                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae is a characteristic of caudal regression syndrome, which is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of the lower half of the body. It is associated with a small pelvis, shallow intergluteal cleft, and club feet. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is caudal regression syndrome?\n2. What are the risk factors for caudal regression syndrome?\n3. Which of the given options is the strongest predisposing factor for caudal regression syndrome in this patient?\n\nAnswer to sub-question 1: Caudal regression syndrome is a rare congenital disorder that affects the development of the lower half of the body. It is characterized by the absence of the sacrum and lumbar vertebrae, which can cause a range of abnormalities in the lower limbs, including clubfoot, hip dislocation, and leg length discrepancy.\n\nAnswer to sub-question 2: The exact cause of caudal regression syndrome is unknown, but it is thought to be related to a combination of genetic and environmental factors. Some of the known risk factors include maternal diabetes, maternal use of certain medications, and exposure to toxins or infections during pregnancy.\n\nAnswer to sub-question 3: Based on the given information, the strongest predisposing factor for caudal regression syndrome in this patient is likely maternal diabetes. Maternal diabetes is a known risk factor for caudal regression syndrome, and the fact that the mother had no prenatal care suggests that her diabetes may not have been well-controlled during pregnancy. However, it is important to note that other factors, such as chromosome abnormalities, could also contribute to the development of caudal regression syndrome in this case.",
