                        "id": "80d8f73e-40da-4535-a336-32efe71561a4",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what a thyroid nodule is?\n\nProfessor: A thyroid nodule is a growth or lump that forms in the thyroid gland, which is located in the neck and produces hormones that regulate metabolism.\n\nStudent: What does it mean if the nodule is fixed, immobile, and non-tender?\n\nProfessor: If the nodule is fixed, it means that it is attached to surrounding tissues and cannot be moved. If it is immobile, it means that it cannot be moved by swallowing or other movements. If it is non-tender, it means that it is not painful to the touch.\n\nStudent: What is a hypoechoic nodule?\n\nProfessor: A hypoechoic nodule is a nodule that appears darker on an ultrasound image, indicating that it is more solid than the surrounding tissue.\n\nStudent: What is neoplasia?\n\nProfessor: Neoplasia is the abnormal growth of cells that can lead to the formation of a tumor.\n\nStudent: What is radioiodine therapy?\n\nProfessor: Radioiodine therapy is a treatment for thyroid cancer that involves taking a radioactive form of iodine, which is absorbed by the thyroid gland and destroys any remaining cancer cells.\n\nStudent: Why is a CT scan performed?\n\nProfessor: A CT scan is performed to check for any signs of cancer that may have spread to other parts of the body, such as the bones or lungs.\n\nStudent: Why is near-total thyroidectomy necessary?\n\nProfessor: Near-total thyroidectomy is necessary to remove as much of the thyroid gland as possible, which reduces the risk of cancer recurrence and makes radioiodine therapy more effective.\n\nStudent: So, which of the following is the most likely initial metastatic site in this patient?\n\nProfessor: The most likely initial metastatic site in this patient is C) Cervical lymph nodes, as the cancer is located in the thyroid gland, which drains into the cervical lymph nodes.",
