                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms: episodic pelvic pain radiating to her back and thighs a few hours before her menstrual period and lasting for 2 days.\n2. Identify the patient's medical history: regular 28-day intervals since menarche at the age of 12 years, sexually active with two male partners, uses condoms inconsistently.\n3. Perform a physical examination to rule out any abnormalities.\n4. Consider the possible causes of the patient's symptoms:\n- Endometrial sloughing and uterine contractions mediated by prostaglandin: This is a common cause of menstrual cramps and is likely in this patient.\n- Ascending infection of the uterus, fallopian tubes, ovaries, or surrounding tissue: This is less likely given the patient's lack of physical exam findings.\n- Fluid-filled sac within the ovary: This is a possible cause, but less likely given the patient's symptoms.\n- Pregnancy: This is unlikely given the patient's regular menstrual cycles and use of condoms.\n- Hormone-sensitive smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium: This is unlikely given the patient's lack of physical exam findings.\n5. Choose the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms based on the above considerations.\n\nAnswer: A) Endometrial sloughing and uterine contractions mediated by prostaglandin.",
