                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 15-year-old girl with episodic pelvic pain that occurs a few hours before her menstrual period and lasts for 2 days. The pain has been occurring for 4 months and is relieved by ibuprofen. She has regular menstrual cycles and is sexually active with two male partners, using condoms inconsistently. Physical examination shows no abnormalities. \n\nThe most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is endometrial sloughing and uterine contractions mediated by prostaglandin. This is a common cause of menstrual cramps, which typically occur a few hours before the onset of menstruation and last for 1-3 days. Ibuprofen is an effective treatment for menstrual cramps because it inhibits the production of prostaglandins. \n\nAscending infection of the uterus, fallopian tubes, ovaries, or surrounding tissue is less likely because the patient has no signs of infection on physical examination and her symptoms are cyclical and related to her menstrual cycle. \n\nA fluid-filled sac within the ovary (ovarian cyst) is also less likely because the patient has no signs of an ovarian mass on physical examination and her symptoms are cyclical and related to her menstrual cycle. \n\nPregnancy is unlikely because the patient is using condoms inconsistently and has regular menstrual cycles. \n\nA hormone-sensitive smooth muscle tumor of the myometrium (leiomyoma) is also less likely because the patient has no signs of a uterine mass on physical examination and her symptoms are cyclical and related to her menstrual cycle. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is endometrial sloughing and uterine contractions mediated by prostaglandin.",
