                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with a chronic cough and shortness of breath, which has worsened over the last 2 days with the onset of fever, body aches, and headache. The physical examination reveals diminished breath sounds bilateral, a barrel-shaped chest, and measured breathing through pursed lips. The chest X-ray reveals a flattened diaphragm and no signs of consolidation. Pulmonary function testing reveals an FEV1/FVC ratio of 60%. These findings suggest chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) with an acute exacerbation, which is commonly caused by a respiratory infection.\n\nThe ABG of this patient is most likely to reveal a primary respiratory acidosis. The patient has a history of smoking, which is a major risk factor for COPD. The physical examination findings, chest X-ray, and pulmonary function testing results are consistent with COPD. The flattened diaphragm on the chest X-ray suggests hyperinflation of the lungs, which is a characteristic feature of COPD. The FEV1/FVC ratio of 60% indicates airflow limitation, which is also a hallmark of COPD.\n\nIn COPD, the airways are narrowed, which leads to impaired gas exchange and retention of carbon dioxide (CO2) in the lungs. This results in respiratory acidosis, which is characterized by an increase in arterial CO2 levels and a decrease in arterial pH. The patient's respiratory rate of 18/min is within the normal range, which suggests that he is not hyperventilating to compensate for the respiratory acidosis. Therefore, the ABG of this patient is most likely to reveal a primary respiratory acidosis (option B).\n\nOption A (non-anion gap metabolic acidosis) is unlikely because there is no evidence of metabolic acidosis in this patient. Option C (primary respiratory alkalosis) is unlikely because the patient's respiratory rate is not elevated, which is necessary to compensate for respiratory alkalosis. Option D (compensatory respiratory acidosis) is unlikely because the patient's respiratory rate is not elevated, which suggests that he is not compensating for the respiratory acidosis. Option E (anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis) is unlikely because there is no evidence of metabolic acidosis or respiratory alkalosis in this patient.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "9646243b-fe23-40ae-b2d9-ce6b874449ce",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Primary respiratory acidosis.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
