                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely result of arterial blood gases (ABG) for this patient?\n\nStep-by-step:\n1. The patient is a 60-year-old man with a 2-day history of increasing difficulty in breathing with a productive cough.\n2. He has a past medical history of prediabetes and a 30-pack-year smoking history.\n3. Physical examination reveals diminished breath sounds bilateral, a barrel-shaped chest, and measured breathing through pursed lips.\n4. A chest X-ray reveals a flattened diaphragm and no signs of consolidation.\n5. Pulmonary function testing reveals FEV1/FVC ratio of 60%.\n6. The question is asking about the most likely result of arterial blood gases (ABG) for this patient.\n\nAnswer: B) Primary respiratory acidosis\n\nReasoning:\n1. The patient has a chronic cough and shortness of breath, which suggests a respiratory problem.\n2. The physical examination findings of diminished breath sounds bilateral, a barrel-shaped chest, and measured breathing through pursed lips also suggest a respiratory problem.\n3. The chest X-ray reveals a flattened diaphragm, which is a sign of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).\n4. The pulmonary function testing reveals a decreased FEV1/FVC ratio, which is also consistent with COPD.\n5. Arterial blood gases (ABG) are used to assess the patient's acid-base balance and oxygenation status.\n6. In primary respiratory acidosis, the pH is decreased and the partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO2) is increased, which is consistent with the patient's respiratory symptoms and physical examination findings.\n7. The patient's blood pressure, pulse rate, and temperature are within normal limits, which do not suggest a metabolic problem.\n8. There is no evidence of metabolic acidosis or alkalosis, which rules out options A, C, and E.\n9. Compensatory respiratory acidosis occurs when the body tries to compensate for a metabolic acidosis, which is not the case for this patient.",
                        "answers": [
