                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with symptoms of fatigue, weakness, abdominal pain, constipation, and weight loss, along with a bronze discoloration of the skin. These symptoms are suggestive of adrenal insufficiency, which can be caused by a deficiency in cortisol (a glucocorticoid) and/or aldosterone (a mineralocorticoid).\n\nThe next best step in the management of this patient would be to confirm the diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency. This can be done by ordering an ACTH stimulation test (option E), which measures the adrenal glands' response to adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). If the test results indicate adrenal insufficiency, appropriate treatment can be initiated based on the specific hormone deficiencies identified.\n\nAdministering intravenous hydrocortisone (option A) or fludrocortisone (option B) without first confirming the diagnosis may not be appropriate, as it could potentially mask the underlying cause of the patient's symptoms. Starting the patient on androgen replacement treatment (option C) would not address the potential cortisol and/or aldosterone deficiencies causing his symptoms. Starting the patient on both hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone therapy (option D) would also be premature without first confirming the diagnosis through an ACTH stimulation test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Order an ACTH stimulation test.",
