                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is presenting with symptoms of fatigue, weakness, abdominal pain, constipation, weight loss, and bronze skin discoloration. These symptoms may indicate a possible adrenal insufficiency or Addison's disease.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Addison's disease?\n2. What are the causes of Addison's disease?\n3. What are the symptoms of Addison's disease?\n4. How is Addison's disease diagnosed?\n5. What is the treatment for Addison's disease?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Addison's disease is a rare disorder that occurs when the adrenal glands do not produce enough cortisol and aldosterone hormones.\n2. The causes of Addison's disease include autoimmune disorders, infections, cancer, and genetic mutations.\n3. The symptoms of Addison's disease include fatigue, weakness, weight loss, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, low blood pressure, and skin discoloration.\n4. Addison's disease is diagnosed through blood tests to measure cortisol and aldosterone levels, as well as an ACTH stimulation test to evaluate adrenal gland function.\n5. The treatment for Addison's disease involves hormone replacement therapy with glucocorticoids (such as hydrocortisone) and mineralocorticoids (such as fludrocortisone).\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings, the next best step in the management of this patient would be to order an ACTH stimulation test (option E) to confirm the diagnosis of Addison's disease. Once the diagnosis is confirmed, the patient can be started on hormone replacement therapy with hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone (option D).",
