                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question and the context\nThe question is asking which assay a hospital would use to detect the genetic material of HIV if a newborn baby may have been infected due to the mother's haphazard use of anti-retroviral medications. This suggests that the hospital is trying to determine if the baby has been infected with HIV.\n\nStep 2: Understand the options\nA) Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) - This test detects antibodies to HIV, not the genetic material of the virus.\nB) Western blot - This test is used to confirm the presence of HIV antibodies, not the genetic material of the virus.\nC) Rapid HIV antibody test - This test detects antibodies to HIV, not the genetic material of the virus.\nD) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) - This test amplifies and detects the genetic material (RNA or DNA) of the virus, making it a good option for detecting HIV in infants.\nE) Southern blot - This test is used to detect specific DNA sequences, but is not commonly used for HIV detection.\n\nStep 3: Choose the best answer\nBased on the context and the options, the best answer is D) Polymerase chain reaction (PCR). This test is commonly used to detect HIV in infants because they may not have developed antibodies yet, and it can detect the genetic material of the virus even at low levels.",
