                        "cot": "\n\nThe infant has a productive cough, watery stools, is not gaining weight, and has white plaques in the mouth, which are all suggestive of an infectious etiology. The presence of rales on cardiopulmonary examination and lymphadenopathy further support this. \n\nOption A, karyotyping, is not likely to be helpful in confirming the diagnosis of an infectious disease. \n\nOption B, DNA testing for CFTR mutation, is not relevant to the current presentation. \n\nOption C, fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test, is used to diagnose syphilis, which is not likely to be the cause of the infant's symptoms. \n\nOption D, polymerase chain reaction for viral genes, is a useful diagnostic tool for identifying viral infections. This option is the most likely to confirm the diagnosis in this case. \n\nOption E, T-cell receptor excision circle analysis, is used to evaluate immune function and is not likely to be helpful in confirming the diagnosis of an infectious disease. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Polymerase chain reaction for viral genes.",
