                    {
                        "id": "849e9dec-a70f-4057-a086-99047ce8a3f9",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The infant presented with a productive cough, watery stools, poor weight gain, and oral thrush. She has not received any immunizations and has not been evaluated by a physician since birth. She has bilateral rales on cardiopulmonary examination and lymphadenopathy. The most likely diagnosis is a respiratory infection, possibly pneumonia, and a possible gastrointestinal infection. The white plaques on the tongue and palate suggest oral thrush, which is common in infants with weakened immune systems. The low weight and height percentiles suggest malnutrition, which could be due to poor feeding or an underlying medical condition. The elevated temperature, rapid pulse and respirations, and low blood pressure suggest a systemic infection.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What tests can confirm a respiratory infection?\n2. What tests can confirm a gastrointestinal infection?\n3. What tests can confirm malnutrition?\n4. What tests can confirm an underlying medical condition?\n5. What tests can confirm an immune system deficiency?\n\nAnswer:\n1. A chest X-ray and sputum culture can confirm a respiratory infection such as pneumonia.\n2. A stool culture can confirm a gastrointestinal infection.\n3. Blood tests for albumin and other nutrients can confirm malnutrition.\n4. A complete blood count, metabolic panel, and other blood tests can help identify underlying medical conditions such as anemia, thyroid disorders, or liver disease.\n5. A complete blood count and immunoglobulin levels can help identify immune system deficiencies.",
                        "answers": [
