                    {
                        "id": "6685ea50-c30d-47d8-9dea-01dba7573925",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the case of the 3-month-old girl with a cough and watery stools?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the girl has been coughing productively for 5 days and has had several episodes of watery stools over the past month. She is exclusively breastfed every 3-4 hours for 15-20 minutes. She was delivered vaginally at 38 weeks' gestation at home and has not yet been evaluated by a physician. The mother reports that her child is not gaining weight. The infant is at the 5th percentile for height and weight. The infant has not received any immunizations. Her temperature is 38.5\u00b0C (101.3\u00b0F), pulse is 155/min, respirations are 45/min, and blood pressure is 88/50 mm Hg. Oral examination shows white plaques covering the tongue and the palate. Rales are heard bilaterally on cardiopulmonary examination. Cervical and inguinal lymphadenopathy is present.\n\nStudent: What is the most likely diagnosis for this case?\n\nProfessor: Based on the symptoms, the most likely diagnosis is a severe case of pneumonia.\n\nStudent: How can we confirm the diagnosis?\n\nProfessor: The most likely test to confirm the diagnosis is a Polymerase chain reaction for viral genes.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what a Polymerase chain reaction is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, a Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a laboratory technique used to amplify a specific DNA sequence. In this case, we would be looking for the presence of viral genes in the patient's sample.\n\nStudent: How does the PCR test work?\n\nProfessor: The PCR test works by using a special enzyme called DNA polymerase to make copies of a specific DNA sequence. The process involves heating and cooling the sample to denature and anneal the DNA strands, respectively. This results in the amplification of the target DNA sequence, which can then be detected and analyzed.\n\nStudent: How accurate is the PCR test?\n\nProfessor: The PCR test is highly accurate and sensitive, with a low risk of false positives or false negatives. However, it is important to note that the accuracy of the test depends on the quality of the sample and the expertise of the laboratory performing the test.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining, professor.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "4f07a050-4dd3-4cbf-8c24-8836e780a359",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Polymerase chain reaction for viral genes.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
