                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is presenting with symptoms of depression, including low mood, difficulty sleeping, poor appetite, low concentration, and low energy. She has a history of similar episodes in high school and a history of binge eating and purging behavior. The physician offers to prescribe a medication for her current symptoms.\n\nTo answer the question, we need to consider which medication should be avoided in this patient. The medication should not worsen her symptoms or trigger any past behaviors.\n\nA) Duloxetine is a selective serotonin and norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SNRI) used to treat depression and anxiety. It may be a good option for this patient, as it can improve mood and energy levels. However, it may also increase anxiety and cause insomnia, which could worsen her symptoms.\n\nB) Trazodone is a serotonin antagonist and reuptake inhibitor (SARI) used to treat depression and insomnia. It may be a good option for this patient, as it can improve sleep and mood. However, it may also cause dizziness and drowsiness, which could worsen her low energy levels.\n\nC) Bupropion is a norepinephrine-dopamine reuptake inhibitor (NDRI) used to treat depression and smoking cessation. It may be a good option for this patient, as it can improve mood and energy levels. However, it may also increase anxiety and cause insomnia, which could worsen her symptoms. Additionally, bupropion has been associated with an increased risk of seizures, which could be a concern for this patient.\n\nD) Mirtazapine is a noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant (NaSSA) used to treat depression and anxiety. It may be a good option for this patient, as it can improve mood and appetite. However, it may also cause drowsiness and weight gain, which could worsen her low energy levels and binge eating behavior.\n\nE) Citalopram is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) used to treat depression and anxiety. It may be a good option for this patient, as it can improve mood and concentration. However, it may also cause insomnia and agitation, which could worsen her symptoms.\n\nBased on the above analysis, the medication that should be most avoided in this patient is D) Mirtazapine, as it may worsen her binge eating behavior and cause weight gain. The other medications may have some potential side effects, but they are not as likely to trigger",
