                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sinus tachycardia is a normal response to stress or exercise, but can also be a sign of underlying medical conditions such as heart disease or pulmonary embolism. An enlarged cardiac silhouette on chest radiograph can indicate cardiomegaly, which can be caused by a variety of conditions including heart failure, valvular disease, or pericardial effusion. The inability to palpate the radial pulse during inspiration is known as pulsus paradoxus, which can be a sign of cardiac tamponade.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is pulsus paradoxus?\n2. What is cardiac tamponade?\n3. What are the symptoms of cardiac tamponade?\n4. How is cardiac tamponade diagnosed?\n5. What is the treatment for cardiac tamponade?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Pulsus paradoxus is the phenomenon of a decrease in systolic blood pressure during inspiration, resulting in a weaker or absent pulse.\n2. Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency in which fluid accumulates in the pericardial sac, compressing the heart and impairing its ability to pump blood effectively.\n3. Symptoms of cardiac tamponade can include shortness of breath, chest pain, lightheadedness, fainting, and a rapid or weak pulse.\n4. Cardiac tamponade can be diagnosed through physical examination, imaging studies such as echocardiography or CT scan, and measurement of intracardiac pressures.\n5. The treatment for cardiac tamponade typically involves urgent pericardiocentesis, which involves inserting a needle or catheter into the pericardial sac to drain the fluid. In some cases, a pericardial window or surgical intervention may be necessary.\n\nBased on this information, the next step in management of this patient would be B) Pericardiocentesis, as the presence of pulsus paradoxus and an enlarged cardiac silhouette on chest radiograph suggest the possibility of cardiac tamponade. IV Heparin, Chest CT scan, and Insert chest tube are not appropriate treatments for cardiac tamponade, and a Pericardial window would be a more invasive procedure than pericardiocentesis and is typically reserved for cases in which pericardiocentesis is unsuccessful or contraindicated.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "878e27d5-e10e-489b-860a-bd3bf216dfe6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Pericardiocentesis.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
