                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what sinus tachycardia is?\n\nProfessor: Sinus tachycardia is a type of fast heart rate that originates from the sinus node in the heart. It is a normal response to physical activity, stress, or fever.\n\nStudent: What does an enlarged cardiac silhouette mean?\n\nProfessor: An enlarged cardiac silhouette means that the heart appears larger than normal on the chest radiograph. This can be a sign of various heart conditions, such as heart failure or pericardial effusion.\n\nStudent: What is pericardiocentesis?\n\nProfessor: Pericardiocentesis is a procedure in which a needle is inserted through the chest wall into the pericardial sac surrounding the heart to remove excess fluid or blood.\n\nStudent: Why would IV Heparin be a possible next step?\n\nProfessor: IV Heparin is a blood thinner that can be used to prevent blood clots from forming in the heart or lungs. It may be considered if the patient has a history of blood clots or if there is suspicion of a clot causing the symptoms.\n\nStudent: What is a pericardial window?\n\nProfessor: A pericardial window is a surgical procedure in which a small opening is made in the pericardial sac to drain excess fluid or blood. It is usually done if pericardiocentesis is not effective or if the fluid or blood keeps accumulating.\n\nStudent: So, what should be the next step in management of this patient?\n\nProfessor: Based on the symptoms described, the most appropriate next step would be B) Pericardiocentesis to remove any excess fluid or blood that may be causing the symptoms.",
