                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the innate immune system is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the innate immune system is the body's first line of defense against infections. It includes physical barriers like the skin and mucous membranes, as well as immune cells like neutrophils and macrophages that can quickly respond to invading pathogens.\n\nStudent: What is an opsonin?\n\nProfessor: An opsonin is a molecule that can bind to a pathogen and mark it for destruction by immune cells. It helps the immune system recognize and eliminate the pathogen more efficiently.\n\nStudent: How does the complement system work?\n\nProfessor: The complement system is a group of proteins that can be activated in response to an infection. It can help to destroy pathogens directly by creating pores in their cell membranes, and it can also act as an opsonin to mark them for destruction by immune cells.\n\nStudent: What is C1 esterase inhibitor?\n\nProfessor: C1 esterase inhibitor is a protein that helps to regulate the complement system. It prevents the complement system from becoming overactive and causing damage to healthy cells.\n\nStudent: So, which component is likely deficient in this patient?\n\nProfessor: Based on the patient's history of recurrent infections with different types of bacteria, it's likely that she has a deficiency in a component of the complement system. The answer choice that best fits this description is E) Induces the alternative complement pathway. This pathway is important for fighting off bacterial infections, and a deficiency in this component can lead to recurrent infections.",
