                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 16-year-old boy who has been experiencing fatigue for several weeks, leading to a decline in academic performance and cessation of sports activities. He spends most of his time playing video games and eating cereal. His mother has Hashimoto's thyroiditis and his father has major depressive disorder. Examination shows conjunctival pallor, inflammation and fissuring of the corners of the mouth, and concavity of the finger nails. Laboratory studies show low hemoglobin, low mean corpuscular volume, high red cell distribution width, and normal leukocyte count. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms and laboratory results?\n2. What is the significance of the patient's family history?\n3. What is the appropriate initial step in treatment for this patient?\n\n1. The patient's laboratory results show low hemoglobin, low mean corpuscular volume, and high red cell distribution width, which are consistent with a diagnosis of iron deficiency anemia. The patient's fatigue, decreased academic performance, and cessation of sports activities are also consistent with this diagnosis. The inflammation and fissuring of the corners of the mouth and concavity of the finger nails are also signs of iron deficiency anemia.\n\n2. The patient's family history is significant because his mother has Hashimoto's thyroiditis, which is an autoimmune disorder that can increase the risk of developing other autoimmune disorders, including pernicious anemia and celiac disease, which can cause iron deficiency anemia. His father's major depressive disorder is not directly related to the patient's anemia, but it may be a contributing factor to his fatigue and decreased academic performance.\n\n3. The most appropriate initial step in treatment for this patient is D) Iron supplementation. Iron deficiency anemia is treated with iron supplementation, which can be given orally or intravenously depending on the severity of the anemia. Regular blood transfusions and allogenic stem cell transplantation are not appropriate treatments for iron deficiency anemia. Fluoxetine and methylphenidate are medications used to treat depression and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, respectively, and are not indicated for the treatment of iron deficiency anemia.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ecd04bff-d6a7-447d-adea-8b2fc88eea89",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Iron supplementation.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
