                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what each of the laboratory values mean?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Hemoglobin is a protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen. Mean corpuscular volume (MCV) measures the size of red blood cells. Platelet count measures the number of platelets in the blood, which are important for blood clotting. Red cell distribution width (RDW) measures the variation in size of red blood cells. Leukocyte count measures the number of white blood cells in the blood, which are important for fighting infections.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that the patient has a low hemoglobin level?\n\nProfessor: A low hemoglobin level indicates anemia, which means that the patient's blood is not carrying enough oxygen to the body's tissues. This can cause fatigue and other symptoms.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that the patient has a high RDW?\n\nProfessor: A high RDW indicates that there is a lot of variation in the size of the patient's red blood cells. This can be a sign of different types of anemia.\n\nStudent: Why is iron supplementation the most appropriate initial step in treatment?\n\nProfessor: Iron is necessary for the production of hemoglobin, so if the patient has anemia due to iron deficiency, iron supplementation can help increase the hemoglobin level and improve symptoms. The patient's low MCV also suggests iron deficiency anemia. However, it's important to confirm the diagnosis with further testing before starting treatment.",
                        "answers": [
