                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the cells in each region of the histology slide.\n\n- Region A: contains adipose tissue (fat cells)\n- Region B: contains smooth muscle cells\n- Region C: contains glandular cells (likely endocrine glands)\n- Region D: contains nerve cells (neurons)\n- Region E: contains connective tissue (fibroblasts and collagen fibers)\n\nStep 2: Consider the patient's symptoms and vital signs.\n\nThe patient is experiencing severe headaches, palpitations, and profuse sweating. His blood pressure is elevated, and his pulse is fast. These symptoms suggest a possible diagnosis of pheochromocytoma, a rare tumor of the adrenal gland that secretes excess catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine), leading to hypertension and other symptoms.\n\nStep 3: Identify the region of the histology slide that contains cells related to the suspected diagnosis.\n\nThe adrenal gland is an endocrine gland that produces hormones, including catecholamines. Therefore, the most likely region of the histology slide that contains cells related to the suspected diagnosis is C, which contains glandular cells (likely endocrine glands).\n\nAnswer: C",
