                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Recall the pathophysiology of sickle cell anemia\nSickle cell anemia is a genetic disorder that results in the production of abnormal hemoglobin (HgbS) that causes red blood cells to become sickle-shaped and less flexible. This can lead to blockages in blood vessels, causing pain, organ damage, and other complications.\n\nStep 2: Understand the mechanism of action of hydroxyurea\nHydroxyurea is a medication that is commonly used to treat sickle cell anemia. It works by increasing the production of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF), which is a type of hemoglobin that is normally present in newborns but decreases as a person ages. HgbF is less likely to form sickle-shaped cells than HgbS, so increasing its levels can help prevent sickling and reduce complications.\n\nStep 3: Determine the direct effects of hydroxyurea on hemoglobin physiology\nBased on the mechanism of action of hydroxyurea, we can eliminate options A and B, as hydroxyurea does not directly affect the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin. Option C is correct, as hydroxyurea increases the levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF). Option D is partially correct, as hydroxyurea does decrease the levels of HgbS indirectly by increasing the levels of HgbF. Option E is incorrect, as hydroxyurea actually increases the levels of HgbF.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Increases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF).",
