                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is D) Decreases levels of HgbS.\n\nHere's the reasoning:\n\nSickle cell anemia is caused by a mutation in the beta-globin gene, resulting in the production of abnormal hemoglobin (HgbS). This abnormal hemoglobin causes red blood cells to become sickle-shaped, which can lead to a variety of complications.\n\nHydroxyurea is a medication that is often used to treat sickle cell anemia. It works by increasing the production of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF), which is a type of hemoglobin that is normally present in newborns but is gradually replaced by adult hemoglobin (HgbA) as a person grows older. HgbF is not affected by the sickle cell mutation, so increasing its levels can help to prevent sickling of red blood cells.\n\nIn addition to increasing HgbF levels, hydroxyurea also has a direct effect on HgbS. It works by inhibiting the production of HgbS, which decreases the amount of abnormal hemoglobin in the blood. This, in turn, reduces the likelihood of sickling and the associated complications.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Decreases levels of HgbS.",
