                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the direct effect of hydroxyurea on the hemoglobin physiology of patients with sickle cell anemia?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Patients with sickle cell anemia have a mutation that results in the production of HgbS, which causes their red blood cells to sickle under certain conditions.\n- Hydroxyurea is a medication commonly used to treat sickle cell anemia.\n- To answer the question, we need to know what effect hydroxyurea has on the hemoglobin physiology of these patients.\n- Option A, \"Increases oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin,\" seems unlikely because sickle cell anemia already affects the oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin due to the HgbS mutation.\n- Option B, \"Decreases oxygen carrying capacity of hemoglobin,\" also seems unlikely because reducing oxygen carrying capacity would worsen the symptoms of sickle cell anemia.\n- Option C, \"Increases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF),\" is a known effect of hydroxyurea in patients with sickle cell anemia. HgbF is a type of hemoglobin that is normally present in fetuses but decreases after birth. In patients with sickle cell anemia, increasing HgbF can help reduce the sickling of red blood cells.\n- Option D, \"Decreases levels of HgbS,\" is also a known effect of hydroxyurea. By reducing the amount of HgbS, the medication can help reduce the sickling of red blood cells.\n- Option E, \"Decreases levels of fetal hemoglobin (HgbF),\" seems unlikely because we just established that hydroxyurea actually increases HgbF levels.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Decreases levels of HgbS.",
