                        "cot": "What is the most likely antibody seen in a 38-year-old woman with eye dryness, foreign body sensation in the eyes, mucosal ulceration and atrophy in the oral cavity, and marked lymphocytic infiltration of the minor salivary glands on biopsy of the lower lip?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The symptoms and findings described are consistent with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the exocrine glands, including those that produce tears and saliva.\n- The presence of lymphocytic infiltration in the salivary glands supports the diagnosis of Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome.\n- There are several autoantibodies that can be associated with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, including anti-SS-A (or Ro) and anti-SS-B (or La) antibodies.\n- Anti-SS-B antibodies are more specific for Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome than anti-SS-A antibodies, and are present in about 60-70% of patients with primary Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome.\n- Therefore, the most likely antibody seen in this patient is A) Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody.\n\nAnswer: A) Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody.",
