                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the exocrine glands, leading to dryness of the eyes and mouth. It can also affect other organs such as the lungs, kidneys, and nervous system. The diagnosis is based on clinical features, serological tests, and biopsy findings.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the clinical features of Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome?\n2. What are the serological tests used to diagnose Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome?\n3. What are the biopsy findings in Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome?\n4. Which of the listed antibodies is associated with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The clinical features of Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome include dryness of the eyes and mouth, fatigue, joint pain, and swelling. Other symptoms may include skin rashes, dry cough, and vaginal dryness.\n2. Serological tests used to diagnose Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome include anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies, rheumatoid factor, and antinuclear antibodies (ANA).\n3. Biopsy findings in Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome typically show lymphocytic infiltration of the salivary and lacrimal glands, as well as atrophy and fibrosis of the glandular tissue.\n4. The most likely antibody seen in this patient is A) Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody, which is present in about 60% of patients with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome. Anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies are also commonly seen in Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome. Anti-centromere antibody is associated with limited systemic sclerosis, anti-Jo-1 antibody is associated with polymyositis/dermatomyositis, and anti-Scl-70 antibodies are associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis. Anti-double stranded (-ds)DNA is associated with systemic lupus erythematosus.",
