                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what mucosal ulceration and atrophy mean?\n\nProfessor: Mucosal ulceration means that there are open sores or wounds on the mucous membrane lining the oral cavity. Atrophy means that the tissue is shrinking or wasting away.\n\nStudent: What does lymphocytic infiltration mean?\n\nProfessor: Lymphocytic infiltration means that there is an abnormal accumulation of lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell, in the minor salivary glands.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each of the answer choices mean?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody is an antibody that is commonly found in patients with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, an autoimmune disorder that affects the salivary and lacrimal glands. Anti-centromere antibody is an antibody that is associated with limited systemic sclerosis, a connective tissue disorder that affects the skin, blood vessels, and internal organs. Anti-Jo-1 antibody is an antibody that is associated with polymyositis, an inflammatory muscle disease. Anti-Scl-70 antibodies are antibodies that are associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis, a more severe form of systemic sclerosis. Anti-double stranded (-ds)DNA is an antibody that is commonly found in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus, an autoimmune disorder that can affect multiple organs and tissues.\n\nStudent: Based on the patient's symptoms, which answer choice is the most likely?\n\nProfessor: The patient's symptoms are consistent with Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome, which is associated with Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Anti-Sj\u00f6gren's syndrome type B (SS-B) antibody.",
