                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is presenting with altered mental status, lethargy, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypoventilation, weakness in the lower extremities with absent deep tendon reflexes, dilated and poorly reactive pupils, and disrobing behavior. These symptoms suggest a possible overdose of a medication. The fact that the patient was started on a new drug 10 days ago is also significant. \n\nA) Lisinopril is an ACE inhibitor used to treat hypertension and heart failure. It is unlikely to cause the symptoms described above. \n\nB) Fluphenazine is a typical antipsychotic used to treat schizophrenia and other psychotic disorders. It can cause sedation, hypotension, and extrapyramidal symptoms (such as muscle rigidity and tremors), but it is unlikely to cause hypothermia, hypoventilation, or absent deep tendon reflexes. \n\nC) Tolbutamide is a sulfonylurea used to treat type 2 diabetes mellitus. It can cause hypoglycemia, but it is unlikely to cause the symptoms described above. \n\nD) Levothyroxine is a thyroid hormone replacement used to treat hypothyroidism. It can cause hyperthyroidism if the dose is too high, but it is unlikely to cause hypothermia, hypoventilation, or absent deep tendon reflexes. \n\nE) Atorvastatin is a statin used to treat dyslipidemia. It can cause myopathy (muscle weakness and pain), but it is unlikely to cause the symptoms described above. \n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms is B) Fluphenazine, which can cause sedation, hypotension, and extrapyramidal symptoms. The fact that the patient has a history of schizoaffective disorder also supports this diagnosis. The new drug that the patient was started on 10 days ago is likely the fluphenazine. The patient may have inadvertently taken too much of the medication or may be experiencing an adverse reaction to it. Immediate medical attention is necessary to manage the patient's symptoms and prevent further complications.",
