                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Epigastric discomfort and cramping pain after meals that subside several hours later is a common symptom of peptic ulcers. Peptic ulcers are open sores that develop on the inside lining of the stomach and the upper portion of the small intestine. They can be caused by a bacterial infection, long-term use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), or excessive alcohol consumption. Smoking is also a risk factor for peptic ulcers.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms of duodenal peptic ulcers?\n2. What are the symptoms of choledocholithiasis?\n3. What are the symptoms of pancreatitis?\n4. What are the symptoms of gastric peptic ulcers?\n5. What are the symptoms of gallbladder cancer?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The symptoms of duodenal peptic ulcers include burning pain in the stomach that improves after eating or taking antacids, nausea, vomiting, bloating, and weight loss.\n2. The symptoms of choledocholithiasis include severe pain in the upper right abdomen, nausea, vomiting, fever, and jaundice.\n3. The symptoms of pancreatitis include severe pain in the upper abdomen that may radiate to the back, nausea, vomiting, fever, and rapid heartbeat.\n4. The symptoms of gastric peptic ulcers include burning pain in the stomach that worsens after eating or taking NSAIDs, nausea, vomiting, bloating, and weight loss.\n5. The symptoms of gallbladder cancer include abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and weight loss.\n\nAnswer: Based on the patient's symptoms and risk factors, the most likely diagnosis is duodenal peptic ulcer (A). The patient's epigastric discomfort and cramping pain after meals that subside several hours later are consistent with the symptoms of peptic ulcers. The fact that she has experienced similar pain in the past and avoids eating suggests that this is a chronic condition. Her smoking and occasional alcohol consumption are risk factors for peptic ulcers. The absence of other symptoms such as severe pain, fever, or jaundice makes choledocholithiasis, pancreatitis, and gallbladder cancer less likely.",
