                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic that targets DNA gyrase, an enzyme involved in DNA replication. Resistance to ciprofloxacin can occur through various mechanisms, including mutations in DNA gyrase, efflux pumps, and modification of target enzymes.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Escherichia coli?\n2. What is ciprofloxacin?\n3. How does ciprofloxacin work?\n4. What are the mechanisms of antibiotic resistance to ciprofloxacin?\n5. Which of these mechanisms is most likely responsible for the survival of the colony?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Escherichia coli is a type of bacteria commonly found in the intestines of humans and animals. While most strains of E. coli are harmless, some can cause illness.\n2. Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone antibiotic that is commonly used to treat bacterial infections.\n3. Ciprofloxacin targets DNA gyrase, an enzyme involved in DNA replication. By inhibiting DNA gyrase, ciprofloxacin prevents bacteria from replicating their DNA and ultimately kills them.\n4. Resistance to ciprofloxacin can occur through various mechanisms, including mutations in DNA gyrase that prevent the antibiotic from binding to the enzyme, efflux pumps that pump the antibiotic out of the cell, and modification of target enzymes that reduce their susceptibility to the antibiotic.\n5. Based on the information given, the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony is mutation of DNA gyrase. Since ciprofloxacin targets DNA gyrase, mutations in this enzyme can prevent the antibiotic from binding and allow the bacteria to survive. The other mechanisms listed (replacement of D-alanine, inactivation by bacterial transferase, production of \u03b2-lactamase, and modification of surface binding proteins) are not directly related to ciprofloxacin resistance.",
