                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure. A microbiologist has isolated several colonies of Escherichia coli on an agar plate. The microbiologist exposes the agar plate to ciprofloxacin, which destroys all of the bacteria except for one surviving colony. The question is asking which of the following is the most likely mechanism of antibiotic resistance in the surviving colony.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. Can you explain what each of the answer choices means?\n\nProfessor: Of course. A) Replacement of D-alanine refers to a change in the structure of the bacterial cell wall. B) Mutation of DNA gyrase refers to a change in the enzyme that is targeted by ciprofloxacin. C) Inactivation by bacterial transferase refers to a mechanism where the bacteria can transfer a gene that inactivates the antibiotic. D) Production of \u03b2-lactamase refers to an enzyme that breaks down certain antibiotics. E) Modification of surface binding proteins refers to a change in the proteins on the surface of the bacteria that the antibiotic targets.\n\nStudent: Thank you, professor. So, which of these mechanisms is the most likely one in the surviving colony?\n\nProfessor: Based on the information given, the most likely mechanism is B) Mutation of DNA gyrase. Ciprofloxacin targets DNA gyrase, and a mutation in this enzyme can prevent the antibiotic from binding to it and killing the bacteria.\n\nStudent: I see. Thank you for explaining it to me, professor.\n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Do you have any other questions?",
