                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what sickle cell disease is?\n\nProfessor: Sickle cell disease is a genetic disorder that affects the shape of red blood cells. Instead of being round and flexible, they become rigid and take on a crescent or sickle shape. This can cause blockages in blood vessels, leading to pain, organ damage, and other complications.\n\nStudent: Why is it important to know that the patient's spleen is surgically absent?\n\nProfessor: The spleen plays an important role in fighting infections, particularly those caused by bacteria. Without a spleen, the patient is at higher risk for infections and may not be able to fight them off as effectively.\n\nStudent: What do the patient's vital signs tell us?\n\nProfessor: The patient's blood pressure is low, which could indicate that he is in shock. His heart rate and respiratory rate are both elevated, which could be a sign of an infection or other underlying condition.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that the patient is lethargic and pale?\n\nProfessor: Lethargy means that the patient is very tired and has little energy. Pale skin can be a sign of anemia, which is common in sickle cell disease and can be worsened by infections.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of the swollen bite marks on the patient's right hand?\n\nProfessor: The bite marks could be a source of infection, particularly if they are red and streaky. It's important to determine what kind of bite caused them in order to determine the appropriate treatment.\n\nStudent: Which of the following bites would be most concerning in this patient?\n\nProfessor: The most concerning bite in this patient would be a spider bite, as some species of spiders can cause severe reactions and even death in people with sickle cell disease. The other bites listed are less likely to cause serious complications in this patient.",
