                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with a severe cough and difficulty breathing, which suggests a respiratory issue. However, he also has a history of a heart attack and continues to consume alcohol despite medical advice. Therefore, it is important to rule out any cardiac issues that may be contributing to his symptoms.\n\nAn ECG and chest X-ray have already been done, but they do not provide enough information to make a definitive diagnosis. Angiography is an invasive procedure that is used to visualize the blood vessels of the heart and is typically reserved for cases where there is a high suspicion of coronary artery disease. A CT scan may be useful in identifying any structural abnormalities in the lungs or heart, but it is not the best option for evaluating cardiac function.\n\nAn echocardiogram is a non-invasive test that uses sound waves to create images of the heart. It can provide information about the size and function of the heart, as well as any abnormalities in the valves or chambers. This test is a good option for this patient, as it can help to determine if his symptoms are related to a cardiac issue.\n\nA stress test is another non-invasive test that is used to evaluate the function of the heart during exercise. It can help to identify any areas of the heart that are not receiving enough blood flow, which may indicate coronary artery disease. However, this test may not be appropriate for this patient, as he is already experiencing significant symptoms at rest.\n\nA cardiac biopsy is an invasive procedure that is used to obtain a small sample of heart tissue for analysis. It is typically reserved for cases where there is a suspicion of a specific cardiac condition, such as myocarditis or cardiomyopathy. This test is not necessary for this patient at this time.\n\nTherefore, the best option for this patient's management is C) Echocardiogram.",
                        "answers": [
