                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is experiencing difficulty breathing and has a history of a heart attack. He also consumes alcohol regularly despite medical advice.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the cause of the patient's difficulty breathing?\n2. What is the purpose of an ECG and chest X-ray in this situation?\n3. What are the potential complications of consuming alcohol after a heart attack?\n4. What is the most appropriate next step in the patient's management?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's difficulty breathing could be caused by a variety of factors, including heart failure, pneumonia, or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD).\n2. An ECG and chest X-ray can help identify any abnormalities in the heart or lungs that may be contributing to the patient's symptoms.\n3. Consuming alcohol after a heart attack can increase the risk of further heart damage and complications, such as arrhythmias and heart failure.\n4. The most appropriate next step in the patient's management would be a C) Echocardiogram, which can provide more detailed information about the structure and function of the heart and help identify any potential issues that may be contributing to the patient's symptoms. Angiography, CT, stress test, and cardiac biopsy may be useful in certain situations, but an echocardiogram is the most appropriate next step given the patient's symptoms and history.",
