                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which ligament must be cut to access the space posterior to the stomach during a laparoscopic enucleation of a pancreatic insulinoma in a 62-year-old woman under general anesthesia?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The surgeon needs to access the space posterior to the stomach to reach the pancreatic tumor.\n- The ligament that connects the stomach to the diaphragm is the gastrohepatic ligament, but cutting it would not provide access to the posterior space.\n- The phrenicocolic ligament connects the left colic flexure to the diaphragm, but it is not relevant to accessing the posterior space.\n- The ligamentum venosum is a remnant of the fetal ductus venosus and is located in the liver, not relevant to accessing the posterior space.\n- The falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and does not provide access to the posterior space.\n- Therefore, the ligament that must be cut to access the posterior space is the phrenoesophageal ligament, which connects the esophagus to the diaphragm.\n\nAnswer: A) Phrenoesophageal ligament.",
                        "answers": [
