                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The pancreas is located behind the stomach and is connected to the duodenum by the pancreatic duct. The gastrohepatic ligament connects the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach, while the falciform ligament attaches the liver to the anterior abdominal wall. The phrenoesophageal ligament connects the esophagus to the diaphragm, while the phrenicocolic ligament connects the left colic flexure to the diaphragm. The ligamentum venosum is a remnant of the fetal ductus venosus.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. Where is the pancreatic insulinoma located?\n- The pancreatic insulinoma is located in the pancreas.\n2. What is the location of the pancreas?\n- The pancreas is located behind the stomach.\n3. What ligaments are in the area posterior to the stomach?\n- The phrenoesophageal ligament, gastrohepatic ligament, phrenicocolic ligament, and ligamentum venosum are in the area posterior to the stomach.\n4. Which ligament must be cut to access the space posterior to the stomach to reach the pancreatic tumor?\n- The correct ligament that must be cut to access the space posterior to the stomach to reach the pancreatic tumor is the gastrohepatic ligament.\n\nAnswer: B) Gastrohepatic ligament",
