                        "cot": "Student: Excuse me, professor. Can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure. We have a 62-year-old woman with a pancreatic insulinoma who needs a laparoscopic enucleation of the tumor. The surgeon needs to enter the space posterior to the stomach to access the tumor. Which of the following ligaments must be cut to access this space?\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. Can you explain what each of the ligaments is?\n\nProfessor: Of course. The phrenoesophageal ligament connects the diaphragm to the esophagus. The gastrohepatic ligament connects the stomach to the liver. The phrenicocolic ligament connects the diaphragm to the colon. The ligamentum venosum is a remnant of the fetal ductus venosus. And the falciform ligament connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall.\n\nStudent: Thank you, professor. So, which of these ligaments needs to be cut?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is B) Gastrohepatic ligament. This ligament needs to be cut to access the space posterior to the stomach and reach the pancreatic tumor.",
