                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most likely cause of the painless right-sided scrotal swelling in a 52-year-old man?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The swelling is painless and started several weeks ago but is not always present.\n- Physical examination shows an 8-cm, soft, cystic nontender right-sided scrotal mass that transilluminates.\n- The mass does not increase in size on coughing and it is possible to palpate normal tissue above the mass.\n- There are no bowel sounds in the mass, and it does not reduce when the patient is in a supine position.\n- Examination of the testis shows no abnormalities.\n- Based on these findings, we can eliminate options C and E, as they are not consistent with the physical examination.\n- Option D, dilation and tortuosity of veins in the pampiniform plexus, is a possible cause of a scrotal mass but is usually associated with pain and is more common in younger men.\n- Option A, imbalance of fluid secretion and resorption by tunica vaginalis, is a possible cause of a scrotal mass but is usually associated with hydrocele, which is a painless swelling of the scrotum.\n- Option B, failure of processus vaginalis to obliterate, is the most likely cause of the mass. The processus vaginalis is a tubular structure that connects the peritoneal cavity to the scrotum during fetal development. Normally, it should close before birth, but if it remains open, it can allow abdominal contents to herniate into the scrotum, causing a painless swelling that transilluminates and does not change with coughing or position.\n\nTherefore, the most likely cause of the painless right-sided scrotal swelling in this 52-year-old man is B) Failure of processus vaginalis to obliterate.",
                        "answers": [
