                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, it is important to note that the patient has a history of asthma, multiple episodes of pneumonia, and elevated IgE levels. These findings suggest the possibility of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA), a condition in which the immune system overreacts to the presence of the fungus Aspergillus in the lungs. \n\nThe patient's symptoms, including coughing up greenish sputum with specks of red, fever, malaise, and brown mucous plugs, also support the diagnosis of ABPA. The chest radiograph shows atelectasis in the right lung base and glove-finger shadows, which are classic findings in ABPA. \n\nThe most appropriate therapy for ABPA is systemic corticosteroids, such as prednisone, to reduce inflammation and suppress the immune response. Isoniazid is used to treat tuberculosis, which is not indicated in this case. Fluconazole and caspofungin are antifungal medications that may be used in some cases of ABPA, but corticosteroids are the first-line treatment. Surgical resection of the infiltrates is not necessary in ABPA. \n\nTherefore, the most appropriate therapy in this patient is D) Prednisone.",
