                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most appropriate therapy for a 22-year-old woman with worsening shortness of breath, a history of asthma, recurrent pneumonia, and elevated serum IgE levels?\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings?\n2. What is the significance of the greenish sputum with specks of red and brown mucous plugs?\n3. What is the significance of the atelectasis and glove-finger shadows seen on chest radiograph?\n4. What is the mechanism of action of each of the listed therapies?\n5. Which therapy is most appropriate for the patient's likely diagnosis and clinical presentation?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The likely diagnosis is allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA), a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus in patients with asthma.\n2. The greenish sputum with specks of red indicates the presence of blood and the brown mucous plugs suggest the presence of fungal hyphae.\n3. The atelectasis and glove-finger shadows seen on chest radiograph are characteristic findings of ABPA.\n4. Isoniazid is an antibiotic used to treat tuberculosis, which is not the likely diagnosis in this patient. Fluconazole and caspofungin are antifungal agents, but they are not effective against Aspergillus fumigatus. Prednisone is a corticosteroid that suppresses the immune system and reduces inflammation, which is the mainstay of treatment for ABPA. Surgical resection of the infiltrates is not indicated in ABPA.\n5. The most appropriate therapy for this patient is prednisone to reduce inflammation and suppress the immune response to Aspergillus fumigatus.",
