                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what betamethasone is and why it is given in this scenario. Betamethasone is a corticosteroid medication that is given to pregnant women who are at risk of preterm delivery. It is given to help mature the fetal lungs and reduce the risk of respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in the newborn.\n\nNow, we need to identify which fetal cells are stimulated by betamethasone. RDS occurs when the lungs of a premature baby are not fully developed and cannot produce enough surfactant, a substance that helps keep the air sacs in the lungs open. Surfactant is produced by type II pneumocytes, so the correct answer is D) Type II pneumocytes. Betamethasone stimulates the production of surfactant by these cells, which helps reduce the risk of RDS in premature infants.",
