                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Raloxifene is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) used to treat and prevent osteoporosis in postmenopausal women. It works by mimicking the effects of estrogen on bone tissue, but it can also have estrogen-like effects on other tissues in the body.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is raloxifene?\n2. How does raloxifene work?\n3. What are the potential side effects of raloxifene?\n4. Which of these side effects is the woman at increased risk for?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Raloxifene is a medication used to treat and prevent osteoporosis in postmenopausal women.\n2. Raloxifene works by mimicking the effects of estrogen on bone tissue, which helps to increase bone density and reduce the risk of fractures.\n3. Potential side effects of raloxifene include hot flashes, leg cramps, increased risk of blood clots, and an increased risk of stroke.\n4. The woman is at increased risk of developing pulmonary embolism, which is a type of blood clot that can travel to the lungs and cause serious complications. This is a known side effect of raloxifene, and the woman's hypertension may also increase her risk of developing blood clots. While raloxifene has been shown to reduce the risk of breast cancer in some women, it is not associated with an increased risk of this condition. Cellulitis, mandibular osteonecrosis, and hypercholesterolemia are not known side effects of raloxifene.",
                        "answers": [
