                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what jaundice is and why it's happening to the boy?\n\nProfessor: Jaundice is a yellow discoloration of the skin and eyes that occurs when there is an excess of bilirubin in the blood. Bilirubin is a waste product that is produced when red blood cells are broken down. In this case, the boy's jaundice is likely due to his recent upper respiratory tract infection, which may have caused his body to break down more red blood cells than usual.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that his spleen tip is palpated 2 cm below the left costal margin?\n\nProfessor: The spleen is an organ located in the upper left part of the abdomen. Normally, it cannot be felt during a physical exam. However, in some cases, such as when the spleen is enlarged, it may be palpable. In this case, the fact that the spleen tip is palpated 2 cm below the left costal margin suggests that the boy's spleen is enlarged.\n\nStudent: What is a Coombs test and why is it important in this case?\n\nProfessor: A Coombs test is a blood test that is used to detect antibodies that are attached to red blood cells. In some cases, such as in autoimmune hemolytic anemia, the body produces antibodies that attack and destroy its own red blood cells. A negative Coombs test in this case suggests that the boy's anemia is not due to this type of autoimmune reaction.\n\nStudent: What is a peripheral blood smear and why is it important in this case?\n\nProfessor: A peripheral blood smear is a test in which a small sample of blood is examined under a microscope to look for abnormalities in the shape, size, and number of blood cells. In this case, the peripheral blood smear is important because it shows that the boy's red blood cells are smaller and more pale than normal, which is consistent with a type of anemia called hemolytic anemia.\n\nStudent: What is splenic sequestration crisis and why is it a potential complication in this case?\n\nProfessor: Splenic sequestration crisis is a complication of sickle cell disease in which a large amount of blood becomes trapped in the spleen, causing it to enlarge rapidly and leading to a sudden drop in hemoglobin levels. This can be life-threatening if not treated promptly. In this case, the boy's enlarged spleen and anemia suggest that he may have sickle cell disease, which puts him at risk for",
