                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's bleeding time is prolonged, indicating a defect in platelet function. The platelet count is normal, ruling out thrombocytopenia. The prothrombin time and activated partial thromboplastin time are within normal limits, indicating normal function of the extrinsic and intrinsic coagulation pathways, respectively. \n\nThe decreased factor VIII activity and ristocetin cofactor assay suggest a deficiency in von Willebrand factor (vWF), which is necessary for platelet adhesion and stabilization of factor VIII. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Gp Ib, which is the receptor for vWF on platelets. \n\nFactor IX deficiency (A) would affect the intrinsic coagulation pathway, but the patient's activated partial thromboplastin time is normal. Factor XI deficiency (C) would also affect the intrinsic pathway, but it is less common and usually does not cause bleeding symptoms unless there is also a deficiency in factor VIII or vWF. Vitamin K deficiency (D) would affect the extrinsic pathway and the production of factors II, VII, IX, and X, but the patient's prothrombin time is normal. Antithrombin III deficiency (E) would affect the activity of multiple coagulation factors, but it is rare and usually presents with thrombotic rather than bleeding symptoms.",
                        "answers": [
