                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient presents with chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, and weight loss. Cardiac examination reveals an irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex. Lung examination shows diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales. The echocardiogram shows a left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40% and a mass in the left atrium.\n\nBased on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is atrial myxoma, which is a benign tumor of the heart that arises from the endocardium. The tumor can cause symptoms such as chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, and weight loss, and can be associated with an irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur. The lung findings may be due to pulmonary edema caused by the tumor obstructing blood flow.\n\nThe biopsy of the mass is likely to show clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity, which are characteristic of atrial myxoma. The tumor is composed of stellate or spindle-shaped cells embedded in a mucopolysaccharide-rich matrix, which gives it a gelatinous appearance. The cells are usually benign and do not show significant mitotic activity or nuclear atypia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity.",
