                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the case.\n\n- 45-year-old woman\n- Intermittent chest pain and palpitations\n- Dyspnea on exertion\n- 4.5-kg (10-lb) weight loss in the past 2 months despite normal appetite\n- No history of serious medical illness\n- Irregularly irregular rhythm and a grade 3/6, low pitched, rumbling, mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex on cardiac examination\n- Diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales in the lung bases\n- Transthoracic echocardiogram shows a left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40% and a mass in the left atrium\n\nStep 2: Determine the likely diagnosis based on the information provided.\n\nThe patient's symptoms and findings suggest a cardiac tumor, most likely a myxoma, which is the most common primary cardiac tumor in adults. Myxomas are usually located in the left atrium and can cause symptoms such as chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea, and weight loss. The murmur heard on cardiac examination is consistent with a myxoma, which can cause a mid-diastolic murmur due to obstruction of blood flow across the mitral valve.\n\nStep 3: Determine the likely histological findings based on the diagnosis.\n\nMyxomas are composed of a mixture of cells from different germinal layers, including stellate and spindle-shaped cells, fibroblasts, and myxoid matrix. The cells are typically bland and lack mitotic activity. The myxoid matrix gives the tumor a gelatinous appearance and consistency.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers.",
