                        "cot": "The patient presents with chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, weight loss, irregularly irregular rhythm, mid-diastolic murmur, wheezing, rales, left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40%, and a mass in the left atrium. Based on these symptoms, it is likely that the patient has a cardiac tumor. \n\nOption A) Nonencapsulated groups of well-differentiated skeletal muscle cells: This is unlikely as it describes a rhabdomyoma, which is a rare benign tumor of skeletal muscle cells that is usually found in children. \n\nOption B) Clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity: This is unlikely as it describes a myxoma, which is a benign tumor of connective tissue cells that is the most common type of primary cardiac tumor in adults. However, the symptoms described in the patient are not consistent with a myxoma. \n\nOption C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers: This is unlikely as it describes a teratoma, which is a rare tumor that contains tissues from all three germinal layers (endoderm, mesoderm, and ectoderm). Teratomas are usually found in the ovaries or testes and are not typically found in the heart. \n\nOption D) Encapsulated cluster of mature fat cells: This is unlikely as it describes a lipoma, which is a benign tumor of fat cells that is very rare in the heart. \n\nOption E) Nests of atypical melanocytes: This is unlikely as it describes a melanoma, which is a malignant tumor of pigment-producing cells that is very rare in the heart. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms and echocardiogram findings is a malignant cardiac tumor, such as a sarcoma or carcinoma. A biopsy of the mass would be necessary to determine the specific type of tumor and its characteristics.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "fe0966a0-355d-4249-8236-302092f9a745",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
