                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the likely biopsy result of the mass in the left atrium of a 45-year-old woman with chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, and weight loss?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient's symptoms suggest a cardiac issue, specifically an irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex.\n- The echocardiogram shows a left-ventricular ejection fraction of 40% and a mass in the left atrium.\n- The presence of diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales in the lung bases suggests possible pulmonary involvement.\n- Given the location of the mass and the patient's symptoms, the biopsy is likely to show a cardiac tumor.\n- The options provided suggest different types of tissue that could be present in the tumor.\n- Option A (nonencapsulated groups of well-differentiated skeletal muscle cells) and option B (clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity) are unlikely as they do not fit with the location or symptoms.\n- Option D (encapsulated cluster of mature fat cells) is also unlikely as it does not fit with the location or symptoms.\n- Option E (nests of atypical melanocytes) is not relevant to the patient's symptoms or location.\n- Option C (mixture of cells from different germinal layers) is the most likely as it suggests a cardiac tumor with multiple cell types, which is consistent with the patient's presentation.\n\nAnswer: C) Mixture of cells from different germinal layers.",
