                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking about the likely biopsy findings of a mass in the left atrium of a 45-year-old woman who presents with chest pain, palpitations, dyspnea on exertion, weight loss, and an irregularly irregular rhythm with a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the left atrium?\n2. What are the possible causes of a mass in the left atrium?\n3. What is the significance of an irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex?\n4. What is the significance of diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales in the lung bases?\n5. What is left-ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) and what is a normal range?\n6. What are the possible biopsy findings of a mass in the left atrium?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The left atrium is one of the four chambers of the heart that receives oxygenated blood from the lungs and pumps it to the left ventricle.\n2. Possible causes of a mass in the left atrium include atrial myxoma (a benign tumor), metastatic cancer, lymphoma, and thrombus (blood clot).\n3. An irregularly irregular rhythm and a mid-diastolic murmur heard best over the apex are suggestive of atrial fibrillation and mitral stenosis, respectively, which can be caused by an atrial myxoma.\n4. Diffuse wheezing and bilateral rales in the lung bases suggest pulmonary edema, which can be caused by heart failure.\n5. LVEF is a measure of how much blood the left ventricle pumps out with each contraction. A normal range is 55-70%.\n6. The likely biopsy findings of a mass in the left atrium with the given clinical presentation are clusters of bland cells without mitotic activity, which are characteristic of an atrial myxoma (answer B).",
                        "answers": [
