                        "cot": "What are the possible side effects of the drug prescribed for a 27-year-old woman with depressed mood, fatigue, and a history of smoking who is actively trying to quit?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The patient is a 27-year-old woman who has been experiencing depressed mood and fatigue for 2 months, along with a 5-kg weight gain. She has trouble concentrating at work and has missed work due to fatigue. She has a history of smoking 2 packs of cigarettes daily for 10 years and is actively trying to quit smoking.\n\n2. The physician prescribes a drug to treat the patient's mood disturbance and support smoking cessation.\n\n3. The question asks about possible side effects of this drug that the patient should be counseled about.\n\n4. Option A, decreased libido, is a possible side effect of some antidepressant medications, which may be prescribed to treat the patient's mood disturbance. However, not all antidepressants cause this side effect, and it may not be relevant to the specific drug prescribed.\n\n5. Option B, hypotension, is a possible side effect of some smoking cessation medications, such as nicotine replacement therapy or bupropion. However, it may not be relevant to the specific drug prescribed for this patient.\n\n6. Option C, urinary retention, is not a common side effect of antidepressants or smoking cessation medications and is unlikely to be relevant to this patient.\n\n7. Option D, tachycardia, is a possible side effect of some smoking cessation medications, such as nicotine replacement therapy or varenicline. However, it may not be relevant to the specific drug prescribed for this patient.\n\n8. Option E, QTc prolongation, is a possible side effect of some antidepressant medications, particularly those in the class of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs). This side effect can lead to a potentially life-threatening arrhythmia called torsades de pointes. Therefore, it is important to counsel the patient about this possible side effect and monitor her ECG if she is prescribed an SSRI.\n\nAnswer: E) QTc prolongation.",
