                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient has a history of asthma and multiple hospitalizations, which are not relevant to the current presentation. The important findings are the sudden loss of consciousness, low glucose and potassium levels, and improvement with dextrose infusion. These suggest hypoglycemia as the cause of the symptoms.\n\nThe overnight episode confirms hypoglycemia, but also shows high insulin and low C-peptide levels. This pattern is consistent with exogenous insulin administration, which can cause hypoglycemia in non-diabetic individuals.\n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is E) Factitious disorder imposed on another, also known as Munchausen syndrome by proxy. This is a rare form of child abuse in which a caregiver (usually a mother) intentionally causes or fabricates illness in a child to gain attention or sympathy. In this case, the mother may be administering insulin to the child to induce hypoglycemia and hospitalization. The low potassium level may be a result of insulin-induced cellular uptake of potassium.\n\nMalingering, glucagonoma, primary adrenal insufficiency, and conversion disorder are less likely diagnoses based on the clinical and laboratory findings.",
