                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient is a 5-year-old boy with a history of asthma and multiple hospitalizations who presents with sudden loss of consciousness, somnolence, and diaphoresis. Serum studies show hypoglycemia (glucose concentration of 22 mg/dL) and hypokalemia (potassium concentration of 2.4 mEq/L). A dextrose infusion is administered, and the patient's symptoms improve. Overnight, the patient has another episode of decreased consciousness, and serum studies taken during the episode show hypoglycemia (glucose concentration of 19 mg/dL), hyperinsulinemia (insulin concentration of 108 mIU/L), and low C-peptide concentration (0.3 ng/mL).\n\nThe most likely diagnosis is insulinoma, a rare tumor of the pancreas that secretes insulin and causes hypoglycemia. The patient's hyperinsulinemia and low C-peptide concentration support this diagnosis. Malingering, conversion disorder, and factitious disorder imposed on another are unlikely in a 5-year-old child. Glucagonoma and primary adrenal insufficiency are also unlikely given the patient's clinical presentation and laboratory findings. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Glucagonoma.",
