                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient is a 5-year-old boy with a history of asthma and multiple hospitalizations. His mother has type 2 diabetes mellitus. He presented with sudden loss of consciousness, low glucose and potassium levels, and improved with dextrose infusion. He had another episode of decreased consciousness overnight, with low glucose, high insulin, and low C-peptide levels.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the significance of the patient's history of asthma and hospitalizations?\n2. What is the significance of the patient's mother having type 2 diabetes mellitus?\n3. What is the significance of the patient's low glucose and potassium levels?\n4. What is the significance of the patient's high insulin and low C-peptide levels?\n5. What conditions are associated with low glucose and potassium levels?\n6. What conditions are associated with high insulin and low C-peptide levels?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's history of asthma and hospitalizations may suggest a chronic illness that could affect his overall health and susceptibility to other conditions.\n2. The patient's mother having type 2 diabetes mellitus may suggest a genetic predisposition to diabetes or other metabolic disorders.\n3. The patient's low glucose and potassium levels may suggest hypoglycemia and hypokalemia, which can cause symptoms such as loss of consciousness and diaphoresis.\n4. The patient's high insulin and low C-peptide levels may suggest hyperinsulinism, which can cause hypoglycemia.\n5. Conditions associated with low glucose and potassium levels include hypoglycemia, insulinoma, and adrenal insufficiency.\n6. Conditions associated with high insulin and low C-peptide levels include insulinoma and exogenous insulin administration.\n\nBased on the information provided, the most likely diagnosis is insulinoma, which is a rare tumor of the pancreas that secretes insulin and can cause hypoglycemia. The patient's high insulin and low C-peptide levels, along with his episodes of hypoglycemia and loss of consciousness, are consistent with this diagnosis. Malingering, glucagonoma, primary adrenal insufficiency, conversion disorder, and factitious disorder imposed on another are less likely diagnoses based on the information provided.",
