                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the question.\n- 59-year-old man with chest pain for 4 hours on the 5th day of hospitalization for acute myocardial infarction\n- Initially diagnosed with ST-elevation myocardial infarction and treated with alteplase\n- Currently has discomfort in chest and left shoulder, similar to initial symptoms\n- Vital signs: pulse 86/min, respiratory rate 16/min, blood pressure 146/90 mm Hg\n- Auscultation of chest reveals no abnormal findings\n- ECG is difficult to interpret\n- Which laboratory test would have the most diagnostic utility?\n\nStep 2: Eliminate irrelevant options.\n- Alanine aminotransferase and alkaline phosphatase are liver function tests and not relevant to the current situation.\n- Lactate dehydrogenase is a nonspecific marker of tissue damage and not specific to cardiac injury.\n\nStep 3: Consider the remaining options.\n- Creatine kinase (CK-MB) and troponin T are both cardiac biomarkers that are elevated in myocardial infarction.\n- CK-MB is less specific than troponin T and can also be elevated in other conditions such as skeletal muscle injury.\n- Troponin T is the preferred biomarker for diagnosing myocardial infarction and has a higher sensitivity and specificity than CK-MB.\n\nStep 4: Choose the best answer.\n- The most diagnostic utility would be provided by troponin T, as it is the preferred biomarker for diagnosing myocardial infarction and has a higher sensitivity and specificity than CK-MB.\n- Therefore, the correct answer is E) Troponin T.",
