                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the question is asking which psychological defense mechanism is consistent with the man's behavior of accusing his wife of flirting and questioning her fidelity when he himself has been flirting with his female co-workers.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what are psychological defense mechanisms?\n\nProfessor: Psychological defense mechanisms are unconscious strategies that people use to protect themselves from unpleasant emotions such as anxiety, guilt, and shame.\n\nStudent: I see. So what is displacement?\n\nProfessor: Displacement is a defense mechanism where a person redirects their negative emotions or impulses from one person or object to another less threatening one. For example, if someone is angry with their boss but cannot express it, they may come home and take out their anger on their spouse.\n\nStudent: And what is dissociation?\n\nProfessor: Dissociation is a defense mechanism where a person disconnects from their thoughts, feelings, memories, or sense of identity to avoid emotional pain or trauma. For example, a person who has experienced a traumatic event may dissociate and feel like they are watching the event happen to someone else.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what is projection?\n\nProfessor: Projection is a defense mechanism where a person attributes their own unacceptable thoughts, feelings, or impulses to someone else. For example, a person who is jealous of their partner may accuse their partner of being unfaithful.\n\nStudent: I understand. And what is reaction formation?\n\nProfessor: Reaction formation is a defense mechanism where a person expresses the opposite of their true feelings or impulses. For example, a person who is attracted to someone of the same sex may express strong anti-gay sentiments.\n\nStudent: And what is repression?\n\nProfessor: Repression is a defense mechanism where a person unconsciously blocks out or forgets unpleasant memories, thoughts, or feelings. For example, a person who has experienced childhood abuse may not remember the abuse until triggered by a similar situation later in life.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining all of that. So which defense mechanism is consistent with the man's behavior?\n\nProfessor: The man's behavior of accusing his wife of flirting and questioning her fidelity while he himself has been flirting with his female co-workers is most consistent with projection. He is projecting his own feelings of guilt and infidelity onto his wife.",
                        "answers": [
