                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most appropriate procedure for elective termination of pregnancy in a 25-year-old woman who has missed her menstrual cycle for 4 weeks, has a positive urine pregnancy test, and desires to discontinue the pregnancy?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient is pregnant and desires to terminate the pregnancy.\n- The question asks for the most appropriate procedure for elective termination.\n- Option A is dilatation and evacuation (D&E), which involves dilating the cervix and removing the contents of the uterus with surgical instruments or suction.\n- Option B is oxytocin-induced labor, which involves using medication to induce contractions and deliver the fetus vaginally.\n- Option C is manual uterine aspiration, which involves using a hand-held syringe to suction out the contents of the uterus.\n- Option D is dilatation and curettage (D&C) after vacuum evacuation, which involves dilating the cervix and using a vacuum to remove the contents of the uterus.\n- Option E is oral mifepristone + misoprostol, which involves taking medication to induce abortion.\n\n- Given the patient's desire for elective termination, options B and D (which involve delivery or removal of the fetus) may not be the most appropriate.\n- Option A (D&E) is a common procedure for elective termination, but may not be the most appropriate for this patient as she has not had any previous surgical procedures and may have a higher risk of complications.\n- Option C (manual uterine aspiration) is a minimally invasive procedure that can be done in an outpatient setting, making it a good option for this patient.\n- Option E (oral medication) is also a good option, but may not be preferred by the patient or may not be available in the clinic.\n\nAnswer: C) Manual uterine aspiration is the most appropriate procedure for elective termination of pregnancy in this patient.",
