                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what gravida 3, para 2 means?\n\nProfessor: Gravida refers to the number of times a woman has been pregnant, including the current pregnancy. Para refers to the number of times a woman has given birth to a baby after 20 weeks of gestation. So, in this case, the woman has been pregnant three times and has given birth to two babies.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of her two previous deliveries being uncomplicated?\n\nProfessor: It means that she did not have any major complications during her previous pregnancies and deliveries, which is a good sign for this current pregnancy.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of her BMI being 33 kg/m2?\n\nProfessor: BMI stands for Body Mass Index, which is a measure of body fat based on height and weight. A BMI of 33 kg/m2 indicates that she is obese, which can increase the risk of complications during pregnancy and delivery.\n\nStudent: What is the significance of her uterus being consistent in size with a 42-weeks' gestation?\n\nProfessor: It means that her uterus has reached the size that is expected at 42 weeks of pregnancy, which is a sign that the baby is fully developed and ready for delivery.\n\nStudent: So, what is the answer to the question?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is D) Meconium aspiration. Meconium is the baby's first stool, which can be released into the amniotic fluid if the baby is stressed or overdue. If the baby inhales the meconium-stained amniotic fluid, it can cause respiratory distress and other complications. Since this patient is at 42 weeks' gestation, her baby is at a higher risk of meconium aspiration.",
