                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to analyze the ECG. The ECG shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with a rate of approximately 180 beats per minute. The QRS complexes are narrow, indicating that the tachycardia is originating from the atria. The P waves are not visible, which suggests that they are buried within the QRS complexes or occurring at a rate faster than the ventricular rate. \n\nBased on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (B). This is a common arrhythmia that can be triggered by excessive alcohol consumption, as in this case. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (A) is a genetic condition that causes thickening of the heart muscle and can lead to arrhythmias, but it typically presents at a younger age and is not associated with alcohol consumption. Brugada syndrome (C) is a rare genetic condition that can cause sudden cardiac death, but it typically presents with a characteristic ECG pattern and is not associated with alcohol consumption. Ventricular tachycardia (D) is a potentially life-threatening arrhythmia that originates from the ventricles and typically presents with a wide QRS complex. Sick sinus syndrome (E) is a condition in which the sinus node, which is responsible for initiating the heartbeat, does not function properly, but it typically presents with a slow heart rate rather than a fast heart rate.",
                        "answers": [
