                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most likely diagnosis for a 28-year-old man who comes to the emergency department with dizziness and palpitations for 2 days after attending a bachelor party where he lost several drinking games, based on the shown ECG?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient is a previously healthy 28-year-old man who attended a bachelor party where he lost several drinking games.\n- He is now experiencing dizziness and palpitations for 2 days.\n- An ECG is shown.\n- We need to determine the most likely diagnosis based on this information and the ECG.\n- Looking at the ECG, we can see that there are irregular QRS complexes and T waves, indicating an arrhythmia.\n- The arrhythmia is not regular, ruling out sick sinus syndrome.\n- The irregularity is not due to a blockage, ruling out hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy.\n- The irregularity is not specific to a certain area of the heart, ruling out Brugada syndrome.\n- The most likely diagnosis is paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, which is a common arrhythmia that can be triggered by excessive alcohol consumption.\n\nAnswer: B) Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation.",
