                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the ECG to me?\nProfessor: Sure, what specifically do you want me to explain?\nStudent: Can you explain what each wave represents?\nProfessor: Of course. The P wave represents atrial depolarization, the QRS complex represents ventricular depolarization, and the T wave represents ventricular repolarization.\nStudent: Okay, thank you. So, what is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?\nProfessor: Based on the ECG, the most likely diagnosis is paroxysmal atrial fibrillation.\nStudent: Can you explain why that is?\nProfessor: Sure. Atrial fibrillation is characterized by an irregularly irregular rhythm with no discernible P waves. In this ECG, you can see that there are no distinct P waves and the rhythm is irregular. Additionally, the QRS complex is normal, which rules out ventricular tachycardia. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy and Brugada syndrome would have different ECG findings. Sick sinus syndrome may have a similar presentation, but the irregular rhythm and absence of P waves make atrial fibrillation more likely in this case.",
