                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What drug would be administered to a 35-year-old woman who presents to the emergency department after losing consciousness at work, experiencing muscle spasms, blurred vision, dry mouth, and hallucinations, and whose physical exam reveals dry red skin, enlarged pupils, and a completely full bladder?\n\nStep 1: The patient is experiencing symptoms after foraging from plants during a hike. What drug would be administered to treat her symptoms?\n\nStep 2: The symptoms suggest anticholinergic toxicity, which can be caused by ingestion of certain plants. The patient's dry mouth, blurred vision, and inability to urinate suggest that her muscarinic receptors are being blocked. The drug that would be administered to treat her symptoms is one that can reverse the anticholinergic effects by blocking the breakdown of acetylcholine, thereby increasing its availability at the muscarinic receptors.\n\nStep 3: The drug that would most likely be administered to this patient to treat her symptoms is B) Physostigmine.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Physostigmine.",
