                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The patient presents with fatigue, generalized itchiness, and jaundice, along with elevated liver enzymes and bilirubin levels. The physical examination shows a palpable liver edge and generalized skin excoriations. The ultrasound shows mildly increased echogenicity of the liver. Based on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is liver disease, possibly cirrhosis. \n\nTo answer the question, we need to identify which of the additional findings is most likely to be found in this patient with liver disease. \n\nA) Inferior vena cava thrombosis: This is not a common complication of liver disease and is unlikely to be found in this patient. \n\nB) Cutaneous xanthomas: These are fatty deposits that can occur in patients with high cholesterol levels, but they are not specific to liver disease. \n\nC) Butterfly-shaped vertebrae: This is a characteristic finding in patients with osteogenesis imperfecta, a genetic disorder that affects bone development. It is not related to liver disease. \n\nD) Increased CA 19-9 levels: CA 19-9 is a tumor marker that can be elevated in patients with pancreatic cancer and other malignancies. While liver disease can increase CA 19-9 levels, it is not a specific finding. \n\nE) Kayser-Fleischer rings: These are copper deposits in the cornea that are seen in patients with Wilson's disease, a rare genetic disorder that affects copper metabolism. While liver disease can be a feature of Wilson's disease, Kayser-Fleischer rings are a specific finding that would suggest this diagnosis. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is E) Kayser-Fleischer rings.",
                        "answers": [
