                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's most likely form of diabetes based on the clinical presentation and diagnostic work-up.\n\nThe patient is a 63-year-old man with a slow and steady weight gain, despite attempts to control his diet and increase his level of exercise. He has an abnormal fasting blood glucose and dyslipidemia, and is diagnosed with diabetes after an oral glucose tolerance test. Based on these findings, the patient most likely has type 2 diabetes.\n\nStep 2: Review the answer choices and eliminate any that are not associated with type 2 diabetes.\n\nA) Strong HLA class II gene makeup: This answer choice is not specific to type 2 diabetes and is more commonly associated with type 1 diabetes. Eliminate.\n\nB) Pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition: This answer choice is associated with type 2 diabetes, but it is not the most likely cause of the patient's diabetes based on the clinical presentation. Keep for now.\n\nC) Pancreatic islet cell leukocyte infiltration: This answer choice is associated with type 1 diabetes and is not likely to be the cause of the patient's diabetes based on the clinical presentation. Eliminate.\n\nD) Auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens: This answer choice is associated with type 1 diabetes and is not likely to be the cause of the patient's diabetes based on the clinical presentation. Eliminate.\n\nE) Unrelated to his abdominal obesity: This answer choice is not consistent with the patient's clinical presentation, as his abdominal obesity is a significant risk factor for type 2 diabetes. Eliminate.\n\nStep 3: Re-evaluate the remaining answer choice and confirm that it is associated with type 2 diabetes.\n\nB) Pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition: This answer choice is associated with type 2 diabetes, specifically in the form of islet amyloid polypeptide (IAPP) deposition in the pancreas. This deposition can lead to beta cell dysfunction and insulin resistance, contributing to the development of type 2 diabetes. Therefore, this answer choice is consistent with the patient's most likely form of diabetes.\n\nAnswer: B) Pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition.",
