                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what dyslipidemia is?\n\nProfessor: Dyslipidemia refers to an abnormal amount of lipids (fats) in the blood. This can include high levels of cholesterol and triglycerides.\n\nStudent: What is an oral glucose tolerance test?\n\nProfessor: An oral glucose tolerance test is a test that measures how well your body can handle a specific amount of glucose (sugar). You drink a sugary solution and then your blood sugar levels are measured at specific intervals to see how your body responds.\n\nStudent: What is BMI?\n\nProfessor: BMI stands for body mass index. It is a measure of body fat based on height and weight. A BMI of 30 or higher is considered obese.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what HLA class II genes are?\n\nProfessor: HLA class II genes are a group of genes that are involved in the immune system. They help the body recognize and fight off foreign invaders like viruses and bacteria.\n\nStudent: What is pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition?\n\nProfessor: Pancreatic islet cell amyloid deposition refers to the buildup of a protein called amyloid in the pancreas. This can lead to damage to the cells that produce insulin, which can cause diabetes.\n\nStudent: What is pancreatic islet cell leukocyte infiltration?\n\nProfessor: Pancreatic islet cell leukocyte infiltration refers to the presence of white blood cells (leukocytes) in the pancreas. This can be a sign of inflammation and can also lead to damage to the cells that produce insulin.\n\nStudent: What are auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens?\n\nProfessor: Auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens are antibodies that the body produces against its own pancreatic cells. This can lead to damage to the cells that produce insulin, which can cause diabetes.\n\nStudent: So, which of the following associations is consistent with this patient\u2019s most likely form of diabetes?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is D) Auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens. This patient's diabetes is most likely type 1 diabetes, which is an autoimmune disease where the body attacks and destroys the cells in the pancreas that produce insulin. The presence of auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens is a hallmark of type 1 diabetes.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "634bc89b-ab11-4cfa-9291-f24aeda12b93",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Auto-antibodies against pancreatic islet cell antigens.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
