                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nA) Lorazepam\n\nThe patient is exhibiting symptoms of opioid withdrawal, including anxiety, nausea, abdominal cramping, vomiting, diarrhea, dilated pupils, diaphoresis, and piloerection. The absence of rebound tenderness or guarding on abdominal exam suggests that there is no acute abdominal pathology. The patient's chronic back pain history suggests that he may have been taking opioids, which could explain his withdrawal symptoms. The most appropriate pharmacotherapy for opioid withdrawal is a benzodiazepine, such as lorazepam, to help manage anxiety and agitation. Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist used for opioid addiction treatment, but it is not appropriate for acute withdrawal management. Methadone and morphine are opioids and would worsen the patient's withdrawal symptoms. Naloxone is an opioid antagonist used for opioid overdose reversal and is not appropriate for withdrawal management.",
