                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What respiratory function change is most likely present in a 76-year-old woman with well-controlled hypertension, who walks one mile daily, has no history of smoking or illicit drug use, and has normal vital signs and physical examination, when compared to a healthy 20-year-old woman?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The question asks about respiratory function changes in the 76-year-old woman compared to a healthy 20-year-old woman.\n- The information provided about the 76-year-old woman includes her history of hypertension, which is well controlled with diet and exercise, and her daily walking routine.\n- Her vital signs are normal, including a pulse of 68/min, respirations of 16/min, and blood pressure of 119/76 mm Hg.\n- Her physical examination shows no abnormalities.\n- We need to determine which respiratory function change is most likely present in this patient compared to a healthy 20-year-old woman.\n- Option A, increased lung compliance, would mean that the 76-year-old woman's lungs are more elastic and easier to inflate than a healthy 20-year-old woman's lungs. This is unlikely, as aging usually leads to decreased lung compliance.\n- Option B, increased vital capacity, would mean that the 76-year-old woman can inhale and exhale more air than a healthy 20-year-old woman. This is also unlikely, as vital capacity tends to decrease with age.\n- Option C, decreased residual volume, would mean that the 76-year-old woman has less air left in her lungs after exhaling than a healthy 20-year-old woman. This is possible, as residual volume tends to decrease with age.\n- Option D, increased chest wall compliance, would mean that the 76-year-old woman's chest wall is more elastic and easier to expand than a healthy 20-year-old woman's chest wall. This is unlikely, as aging usually leads to decreased chest wall compliance.\n- Option E, decreased A-a gradient, would mean that the 76-year-old woman has less difference in oxygen levels between her alveoli and arterial blood than a healthy 20-year-old woman. This is possible, as A-a gradient tends to increase with age due to decreased lung function.\n- Therefore, the most likely respiratory function change present in the 76-year-old woman compared to a healthy 20-year-old woman is decreased residual volume.\n- The answer is C) Decreased residual volume.\n\nRight answer: C) Decreased residual volume.",
